 Chondromalacia patella.\n2. Chronic back pain.\n3. Depression.\n\nPLAN\n\nFor her first problem, right knee pain, I have diagnosed her with chondromalacia patella. She will take anti-inflammatories, ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food. I recommended that she perform straight leg raising exercises to strengthen her quadriceps. We will re-evaluate in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve.\n\nFor her second problem, chronic back pain, I recommend that she continue swimming after her knee is better.\n\nFor her third problem, depression, I recommend that she continue her current medication.\n\nAll questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Rebecca Brown is a 27-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of her pain started 1 month ago after she began swimming more regularly. She denies any injury or trauma to her knee. She notes that she is only having pain in her right knee while swimming. She is using the breaststroke technique with her legs kicking out. She is able to swim 10 laps initially, but after a couple of laps, she starts to feel pain in her right knee. She denies any pain down her leg.\n\nMs. Brown has a past medical history of carpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release, chronic back pain, and depression. She has been taking medication for her depression and has been seeing a therapist. She has been doing well with this. She has been getting outside more and has been doing well with her back pain. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations.\n\nMs. Brown denies any knee pain outside of swimming. She denies any back pain. She denies any pain down her legs. She denies any joint swelling.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nShe is currently taking medication for depression.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight changes.\nEENT: Denies dysphagia.\nPsychiatric: Denies suicidal or ho