k pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is stable and well-controlled at this time.\n• Medical Treatment: She should continue swimming regularly once her knee is feeling better.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her current regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current medications.\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome.\n• Medical Reasoning: Relatively stable status post carpal tunnel release.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen with no changes at this time.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nRebecca Johnson is a 27-year-old female who presents for an evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of her symptoms began approximately one month ago. She has been experiencing moderate pain in her right knee. The pain occurs after 2 laps of swimming and has been progressively worsening. She denies any trauma or injury to the knee. She has been swimming more regularly over the past month.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of carpal tunnel release, status resolved. She also reports a history of chronic back pain and depression.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she is a teacher. She has been swimming regularly over the past month.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nPsychiatric: Reports depression, history of depression.\nNeurological: Denies radicular symptoms.\nGeneral: Reports chronic back pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: No swelling in the joints. Pain with patellar inhibition of the right knee. Normal range of motion of the right knee.\nCV: Regular rate and rhythm.\nRESPIRATORY: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No adenopathy.\nNEURO: Normal strength and sensation. No focal deficits.\nENDO: Thyroid is normal.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Chondromalacia patella.\n2. 