ase that a little bit , okay ? and then want you to check it and i\'m gon na have you come back in a month and we\'ll see how you\'re doing with regards to that , okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , your second problem is your dep- depression , you\'re doing great , i do n\'t wan na change anything at this point . let\'s just stay- ... we\'ll change one thing with your high blood pressure-\n[patient] mm-hmm .\n[doctor] so i do n\'t wan na change any other medications at this point in time , so let\'s just leave that as it is .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] and then your third problem was your carpal tunnel . that seems to be doing well . if you notice any tingling or any changes or you get some of those symptoms back , just let me know . okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] all right . anything else i can do for you today ?\n[patient] no , that\'s it .\n[doctor] okay . great . uh , lem me get you checked out .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] hey dragon , finalize the report .\n\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAnnual exam.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristine Flores is a 63-year-old female presenting for her yearly exam. She has a history of high blood pressure and depression in the past. She also had a right carpal tunnel release done about 2 years ago.\n\nThe patient states that she has been doing well with her depression. She notes that she has been taking her medication as prescribed at her last visit. She states that she has been going to therapy every week, which has helped her manage her symptoms. The patient notes that she is sleeping well at night and has been getting a full 8 hours. She denies any thoughts of self-harm or harming others.\n\nThe patient states that she has been checking her blood pressure at home. She notes that her blood pressure has fluctuated lately. She states that she has not had a normal reading in a couple of weeks. The patient has continued utilizing her medications.\n\nShe states that her diet has been a little all over the place. The patient notes that she has been working more hours. She reports that she has had some headaches, however, she thought that it was related to her increased screen time.\n\nThe patient states that her right carpal tunnel release has been doing well. She denies numbness or tingling in her hands or fingers.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Endorses blood pressure issues.\n• Neurological: Endorses headaches.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neurological/Psychological: Appropriate mood and affect. Bilateral grip strength equal. Neurologically intact.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n\nVITALS REVIEWED\n\n• Blood Pressure: Elevated.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLaboratory studies are all within normal limits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nChristine Flores is a 63-year-old female presenting for her yearly exam.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well and managing this with medication and weekly therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressures have been fluctuating lately. She does admit to dietary indiscretion due to her increased workload.\n• Medical Treatment: Increase lisinopril to 10 mg once daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: She should continue to monitor this at home and follow up in 1 month.\n\nCarpal tunnel release.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well 2 years postoperatively and is asymptomatic at this time.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: She will contact us if her symptoms return.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRoutine check-up.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristine Johnson is a 63-year-old female who presents for a routine check-up.\n\nThe patient reports she has been doing well. She states she has been compliant with her medication for depression and has been seeing a therapist weekly, which has been helping her manage her symptoms. She has been sleeping 8 hours per night. She denies any suicidal ideation. She reports she has had some elevated blood pressures recently. She has been taking her medication as prescribed, but notes her diet has been all over the place lately due to increased work hours. She reports she has not had time to go grocery shopping and has been eating a lot of frozen meals. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She notes some headaches, which she attributes to looking at the computer screen all day.\n\nThe patient reports that she had a carpal tunnel release done 2 years ago on the right wrist. She states she has been doing well since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports sleeping 8 hours per night.\n• Eyes: Denies visual changes.\n• HENT: Denies sore throat.\n• Respiratory: Denies cough.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\n• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, denies dizziness, denies syncope.\n• Psychiatric: Denies suicidal ideation.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• General: Alert and oriented, in no acute distress.\n• Skin: No rashes or lesions.\n• Head: Normocephalic, atraumatic.\n• Eyes: PERRLA, EOMI, PERRL 2+, no xanthelasma.\n• Ears: TMs within normal limits, no cerumen impaction.\n• Nose: No septal deviation, no post-nasal drip.\n• Mouth: No lesions, no halitosis.\n• Neck: Supple, no lymphadenopathy, no thyromegaly.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, no extra heart sounds.\n• Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.\n• Extremities: No edema, no clubbing or cyanosis.\n• Neurological: Cranial nerves II-XII intact, normal strength in all extremities, normal sensation, no focal deficits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nBlood pressure today is 145/90 mmHg.\n\nLabs: All within normal limits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 63-year-old female who presents for a routine check-up.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure today was elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: Increase lisinopril to 10 mg once a day.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her current treatment.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current treatment regimen.\n\nCarpal Tunnel Syndrome.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current treatment regimen.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 month. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRoutine yearly exam.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Christine Thompson is a 63-year-old female who presents for a routine yearly exam. She reports a history of hypertension and depression. She had a carpal tunnel release approximately 2 years ago. The patient reports that she has been doing well with her depression. She is taking her medication and attending therapy weekly. She is sleeping well and denies any suicidal ideations. She notes that she has been checking her blood pressure at home and it has been high and low. She denies any headaches or swelling. She notes that she has been eating poorly lately because of increased work hours.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports carpal tunnel release 2 years ago. Denies numbness or tingling in her hands or fingers.\n\nPSYCHOSOCIAL\n\nMs. Thompson reports that she has been doing well with her depression. She is taking her medication and attending therapy weekly.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n