care or emergency room visits for respiratory issues in the past year. She denies requiring prednisone for breathing issues in the past year.\n\nShe hopes to be enlisted in the military in early spring of 2022. She is currently undergoing exertional testing to this purpose. She explains that this gives hers 6 to 7 months to show that she can exert herself without needing any inhalers.\n\nOver the past year, she admits to vaping and smoking, though she is trying to quit and has cut down her use significantly.\n\nShe notes having a nebulizer but denies needing it recently and has only used it in her past for upper respiratory infections and as a child a few times.\n\nMrs. Hernandez uses Zyrtec during the spring and fall but does not typically have to use it consistently during the summer. She confirms she has been taking Zyrtec 3 days per week when she works outside, and it works well to control her symptoms.\n\nShe has been successfully avoiding peanuts. Incident from last year was reviewed where she was exposed to peanuts when she was cleaning an Enterprise rental car that had a lot of peanuts in it. She developed contact hives when the peanuts encountered her skin. Over the past year she denies additional accidental exposures. She wears long sleeves and changes her gloves between each cleaning. She thinks it is likely that she has been in contact with cars that contained peanuts, but due to her protective measures she did not touch them directly. She confirmed she always keeps an EpiPen with her.\n\nThe patient is also avoiding tree nuts. She did ingest almonds without issue following our discussion that she could try them. Despite tolerating them well, she continues to avoid all tree nuts.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nAsthma.\nAllergy rhinitis.\nFood allergies to peanut and tree nuts.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nCurrently smokes tobacco and vapes, trying to reduce and interested in cessation in preparation for joining the army.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nWixela Inhub 250 mcg-50 mcg/dose powder for inhalation. 1 puff 2 times daily in the morning and evening.\nProair HFA as needed.\nEpiPen as needed.\nAlvesco 80 mcg 2 puff 2 times daily.\n\nALLERGIES\n\nPeanuts cause a severe, anaphylactic reaction.\nMontelukast sodium, “seizure-like” reaction.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAsthma Control Test (ACT) Results: ACT score is 21.\nCurrent food skin test sensitivities include peanuts.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Moderate persistent asthma, uncomplicated.\n• Allergic rhinitis.\n• Anaphylactic reaction due to peanuts.\n\nEmily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female who presented today for her yearly follow-up appointment for asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergy.\n\nPLAN\n\nModerate persistent asthma, uncomplicated\nThe patient has been using Wixela inhaler daily and admits to reducing her daily dose to one puff per day, 3-4 days per week as she feels she does not always need it the full dose. Additionally, she aspires to join the military and she must meet certain physical fitness requirements without the use of her inhaler. I counseled the patient on current asthma treatment guidelines which recommend using Wixela and Alvesco only when needed during periods of illness. The patient agreed to reducing her medicine to Alvesco which is a single medication, versus 2 in Welixa. The patient will continue to use her albuterol inhaler as needed. Her ACT score is 21 today and her spirometry test today was better than it was in 2019, though she recalls that it was smoky from wildfires during her last breathing test.\n\nAllergic rhinitis\nShe will continue to use Zyrtec as needed, primarily during spring and fall and sometimes in summer. Her current dose cadence is 3 times per week when she works outside. Symptoms are currently under control.\n\nAnaphylactic reaction due to peanuts\nThe patient has successfully avoided peanuts. Plan is to continue with current methods to avoid exposure. I will refill her EpiPen which expires annually.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nModerate persistent asthma, uncomplicated\nStart Alvesco, 2 puffs twice daily. Keep Wixela in case Alvesco does not work well. Continue albuterol as needed. Return in 4 months to repeat ACT and spirometry testing.\n\nAllergic rhinitis\nContinue Zyrtec 10 mg daily as needed.\n\nAnaphylactic reaction due to peanuts\nContinue strict avoidance of peanuts. I will refill her EpiPen today.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nYearly follow-up for asthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nEmily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female who presents for her yearly follow-up. The patient was last seen in April 2020. She reports she was switched from Symbicort to Wixela due to insurance changes. She uses Zyrtec in the spring, summer, and fall, which tends to work well. She also has a known peanut allergy, but tolerates almonds and cashews.\n\nThe patient states she has been doing well. She uses her inhaler once in the morning and once at night. She states she rarely uses her albuterol inhaler and only when she is exerting herself. She notes she has been trying to use her daily inhaler less because she is trying to get into the military. She states she is able to go to the grocery store and walk without any issues. She only uses her inhaler before a workout.\n\nShe denies any urgent care or ER visits for breathing over the past year. She denies any episodes of needing prednisone for breathing issues.\n\nThe patient is planning to enlist in the military early spring of 2022. She notes she will need to prove she can operate without an inhaler to be accepted.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, weight loss, or night sweats.\nRespiratory: Denies cough or wheezing. Denies hemoptysis.\nENT: Denies nasal congestion or sinus issues.\nAllergic/Immunologic: Denies rash or itching. Denies any issues with tree nuts other than peanuts.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Auscultation of Lungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezing, rales, or rhonchi.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\nENT: Ears: Bilateral cerumen impaction. Mouth: No lesions or petechiae. Nose: Bilateral nasal septum deviation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nSpirometry: FEV1: 85% of predicted. FVC: 95% of predicted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nEmily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female with a history of asthma, allergic rhinitis, and a peanut allergy, who presents for a yearly follow-up.\n\nAsthma.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her spirometry shows a FEV1 of 85% of predicted. She reports good control with Wixela. She rarely uses her albuterol inhaler. She denies any urgent care or ER visits over the past year. We discussed the option of stepping down to an inhaler, but she prefers to continue Wixela.\n\nAllergic Rhinitis.\n• Medical Reasoning: She continues to use Zyrtec as needed in the spring, summer, and fall. She denies any issues with tree nuts other than peanuts.\n\nPeanut Allergy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She continues to avoid peanuts. She has tolerated almonds and cashews in the past. She has had no recent exposure to peanuts and has been cautious.\n\nPLAN\n\nAsthma.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Wixela 250/50 mcg, 1 puff twice a day. Order Alvesco 80 mcg, 2 puffs twice a day, quantity: 1 inhaler, 30 doses. Order Albuterol, 90 mcg/inhalation, 1 inhaler, 2 refills. Repeat spirometry in 4 months.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the option of stepping down to an inhaler. She will continue to use her daily inhaler less as she is trying to get into the military.\n\nAllergic Rhinitis.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Zyrtec 10 mg as needed in the spring, summer, and fall.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: Continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts.\n\nPeanut Allergy.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue to avoid peanuts. Order Epipen, quantity: 2, for emergency use.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 months with ACT and spirometry, sooner if needed. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAsthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nEmily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female who presents today for a yearly follow-up of her asthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy. Due to insurance changes since her last office appointment in April 2020, she was switched from Symbicort to Wixela. She also uses Zyrtec in the spring, summer and fall for her seasonal allergy symptoms, which tend to work well. She also has known peanut allergy, she does tolerate almonds and cashews.\n\nThe patient last saw me in April 2020 for a telemedicine visit. At that time, she was on Symbicort. She has been doing well since that time.\n\nThe patient is currently on Wixela 200/50, one puff twice a day. She reports that it works well for her. She also uses her albuterol inhaler rarely, only before a workout or strenuous activity. She is currently trying to reduce her daily use of Wixela since she is trying to get into the military soon. She has been trying to use her daily inhaler less and less. She has been doing well on this regimen. She denies any urgent care or ER visits for breathing over the past year.\n\nRegarding her allergies, she typically uses her Zyrtec in the spring and fall. She is currently in the midst of spring, and she has been taking it on days when she knows she will be outside a lot. She works outside 3 days a week, so she takes it on those days. She reports that it works well for her.\n\nRegarding her peanut allergy, she is still avoiding peanuts. She has had some testing done in the past for tree nuts, but she does tolerate almonds and cashews. She has been careful about avoiding all tree nuts. She has not had any reactions since we last saw her. She has an EpiPen that she keeps with her.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or changes in appetite.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath, wheezing or coughing.\nSkin: Denies rashes or hives.\nAllergic: Denies anaphylaxis.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth and Throat: Ears: Bilateral cerumen. Neck: No lymphadenopathy.\nRespiratory: Lungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales or rhonchi.\n\nRESULTS\n\nBreathing test results are reviewed and are comparable to her prior results from 2019. Her numbers have actually improved since that time.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAsthma.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well on her current regimen and her breathing test results are comparable to her prior results from 2019.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue using Wixela 200/50 one puff twice a day. I will call in a new Alvesco inhaler, 80 mcg, two puffs twice a day for her to try. We will repeat her breathing test in 4 months to document improvement.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to use the Alvesco only when she is ill or during smoke season. She should also use her albuterol inhaler as needed.\n\nAllergic Rhinitis.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her symptoms are well controlled on Zyrtec.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue to use over the counter Zyrtec 10 mg as needed.\n\nPeanut Allergy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well and avoiding peanuts.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts. She has an EpiPen that she keeps with her.\n\nAllergies.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well on her current regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue to use her albuterol inhaler as needed. Continue to use her Zyrtec as needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue to use the Wixela 200/50 one puff twice a day. Continue to use the albuterol inhaler as needed. Continue to use the Zyrtec as needed. Continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts. Follow up in 4 months for a breathing test and spirometry. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for asthma, allergic rhinitis, 