k, but also stated he consumed several cups of coffee on an empty stomach the morning of. He denies additional episodes of palpitations.\n\nHe has been monitoring his blood pressure at home daily, obtaining systolic blood pressure readings of 146 to 155. He does report a family history of hypertension. Additionally, he has been monitoring his salt intake and feels he is doing well at reducing it. He also walks on his treadmill as often as he can.\n\nHe has a new primary care physician who started him on a new medication Benicar HCT, which he has been using for the past month.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nSeveral family members positive for hypertension.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nBenicar HCT 40 mg/25 mg\nAmlodipine 10 mg daily.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nCardiovascular\nNormal rate, regular rhythm, S2 normal, normal heart sounds, and normal pulses.\n\nPulmonary/Chest\nBreath sounds normal. \n\nAbdominal\nThere is no tenderness.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nThere is no edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThyroid profile 03/01/2021.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Resistant hypertension.\n\nMr. Roberts presents with resistant hypertension, with systolic blood pressure readings between 146-155. He is currently on maximal doses for 3 medications.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have added spironolactone to his regimen which will require monitoring of his potassium and sodium. We will schedule him for a follow up in 2 weeks to complete a basic metabolic panel and check his blood pressure. Next medication to consider adding is carvedilol. I have tried to avoid this, given the potential for issues with erectile dysfunction. I counseled the patient about spironolactone side effects, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances, as well as his increased risk of dehydration, with one sign to watch for being dizziness. If we are not able to improve his blood pressure with spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to the resistant hypertension clinic.\n\nI provided and reviewed with Mr. Roberts the American Heart association website (heart.org) and encouraged him to access for tips on lowering his salt intake and increasing his potassium intake. His maximum salt intake should be 1500 to <2000 mg per day. He was provided materials to follow the DASH diet and encouraged to meet 30-min of walking most days.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSchedule follow up appointment in 2 weeks for blood pressure check and basic metabolic panel. Reduce sodium intake to 1500 to <2000 mg per day. Follow DASH diet.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension and palpitations.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the management of hypertension. He was recently seen in the emergency room for elevated blood pressure and palpitations. The patient states he has a history of hypertension. He has been feeling fine and has not experienced any episodes of palpitations since his last visit.\n\nThe patient reports that he has had issues with hypertension since he was young. He states he has a family history of hypertension. He states he thought his blood pressure was better and he has a new primary care physician who placed him on a new medication one month ago.\n\nThe patient is currently taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily and Benicar HCT 20-5 mg daily. He is taking his blood pressure daily and reports his blood pressure has been running 146-155. He is trying to limit his salt intake and is trying to walk on the treadmill when he can.\n\nGENDER\n\nMale.\n\nAGE\n\n49.\n\nDATE OF BIRTH\n\n03/02/1972.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he is married with two children. He is a truck driver and works night shifts. He is a college graduate and enjoys walking on the treadmill and playing video games.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPositive for hypertension.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 10 mg daily.\nBenicar HCT 20-5 mg daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nHeart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nLungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPotassium 4.0 mmol/L, 03/01/2021.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nResistant hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have added spironolactone to his regimen. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. Next medication would be carvedilol, which I would like to avoid giving, it\'s potential for erectile dysfunction. I have talked to the patient about that and the potential side effects of spironolactone, including the gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. If he is not controlled on spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to a resistant hypertension clinic.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks for a blood pressure check and metabolic panel. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPalpitations and hypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his hypertension and palpitations. The patient was seen in the emergency room for palpitations and high blood pressure. The patient states that he has had issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension. He has a new primary care doctor who started him on a new medication 1 month ago. He is currently taking Benicar HCT 40-25, which he calls his “water pill”, and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He has been checking his blood pressure at home and has been running 146-155 mmHg. He is trying to reduce his salt intake and is doing better. He has been walking on the treadmill when he can. He denies any recent episodes of high blood pressure.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nBenicar HCT 40-25 mg daily.\nAmlodipine 10 mg daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\n- General Examination: In no apparent distress.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops or rubs, and no S3 or S4.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThyroi