terested in Naltrexone injections. She acknowledges that her blood pressure would likely improve if she stopped alcohol consumption. She is checking her blood pressure in the morning and evening, stating it is higher at night but will improve after sitting for a few minutes. She is averaged 135-145 or 150 over 95-110.\n\nShe experienced side effects from lisinopril, such as headaches, “swooshing” in her left ear, and lethargy. She took half a dose and did not experience the side effects.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\nHypercholesterolemia.\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 20 mg tablet once daily.\nHydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg once daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nNo distress. No fever. No tobacco or other substance use.\n\nNeurological\nAlert.\n\nPsychological\nShe does not appear anxious, agitated or depressed.\n\nNeck\nJVP flat with a head at 90 degrees.\n\nRespiratory\nNo cough or shortness of breath. Chest clear to percussion and auscultation. Unlabored breathing.\n\nCardiovascular\nNo CVA tenderness. Cardiac rhythm regular. No murmur. No gallop.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nNo ankle edema.\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin is moist. Good trigger.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Alcohol use disorder.\n• Hypertension.\n\nMrs. Taylor presents for a follow-up for hypertension. History includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on April 16, BP was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nPLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder\nI will start her on naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on May 17. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to the patient.\n\nHypertension\nShe will continue taking lisinopril 10 mg/day and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve significantly if she is able to completely stop consuming alcohol. Target BP is 130-140/70-80. I will see her back week of June 14 which likely will be the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nRecommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2, she defers this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic with any questions. Make follow up appointment week of June 14th.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for hypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up of hypertension. She has a history of significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 today, hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily were prescribed. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports struggling with alcohol cessation. She had been drinking 4 to 5 times a week of hard alcohol. She is willing to stop drinking. The patient is taking half of the lisinopril dose due to feeling lethargic and tired. She is taking the full dose of hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, shortness of breath, or any other symptoms. She is checking her blood pressure at home and reports morning readings of 130-145/95-100 and evening readings of 150/100. She takes her medications in the morning.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypercholesterolemia.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nShe last smoked on 12/25/2017. She drinks alcohol 4 to 5 times a week. She has not had a drink since Monday. She received her COVID-19 vaccine on 02/09/2021.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, weight loss, or fatigue.\nRespiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath, or wheezing.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or edema.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or constipation.\nGenitourinary: Denies dysuria, frequency, or hematuria.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle aches.\nSkin: Denies rashes or lesions.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: Alert, pleasant, in no acute distress.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. Unlabored breathing.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No JVD. CVA tenderness absent. No edema.\nNeurological: No focal deficits. No tremors.\nSkin: No jaundice or cyanosis. Skin is moist.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is drinking alcohol 4 to 5 times a week to excess.\n• Medical Treatment: She would like to start Naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure has been uncontrolled today at 150/100. She is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130-140/70-80.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in the week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection. She was advised to upgrade her influenza vaccination and Shingrix.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up hypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. Her history is significant for significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04-16-2021 her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been drinking for 4 to 5 days a week, hard alcohol. She is taking her medications as prescribed. She reports that the first time she took the lisinopril she felt lethargic and tired, so she only took a half dose the next time. She reports that she feels much better and that it is definitely helping but that she does not know that it will be okay until she stops drinking. She is taking her blood pressure medications. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, shortness of breath, or any other symptoms. She is doing home blood pressure checks. She is taking the lisinopril and the hydrochlorothiazide in the morning. She reports that her blood pressure is lower in the morning than in the evening. She has been taking her medications at the same time each day. She has tried medications in the past and she thinks that this may be the one that works.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nSignificant alcohol use disorder.\nMild hypercholesterolemia.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nShe reports that she drinks 4 to 5 days a week.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily.\nHydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNone to report.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nAlert, pleasant, in no acute distress.\n\nCardiovascular\nNo CVA tenderness. Regular rate and rhythm. No murmur, gallop, or rub.\n\nRespiratory\nClear to auscultation bilaterally. Unlabored breathing.\n\nSkin\nMoist.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. Her history is significant for significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04-16-2021 her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed