t knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. We performed a fluid aspiration of the right knee today, see procedure note above. A steroid injection to the right knee was given today, see procedure note above. We will arrange for x-ray of the right knee and refer his to physical therapy.\n\nLower extremity pitting edema.\nThe patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not seen his PCP. We discussed compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. He will check with his PCP for further instructions.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his rheumatoid arthritis. He has been experiencing right knee pain and swelling. The pain has been present for the past 2 months. He rates his pain as 6 out of 10. He also reports that his left leg has been swollen. The patient states that he has been experiencing this pain and swelling daily. He states that the pain and swelling have impacted his ability to walk and to sit for long periods. He has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. He has been compliant with his medication regimen. He has been wearing compression stockings but reports that they have not helped with the swelling. He received a steroid injection in his right knee last time he was here, which provided temporary relief. He has not had any relief since that time.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and swelling. Denies left knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Normal capillary refill in the toes.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Pitting edema around the right knee and ankle. Range of motion slightly diminished. The right knee is hot to touch.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nTyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his rheumatoid arthritis. He has been experiencing right knee pain and swelling. The pain has been present for the past 2 months. He rates his pain as 6 out of 10. He also reports that his left leg has been swollen. The patient states that he has been experiencing this pain and swelling daily. He states that the pain and swelling have impacted his ability to walk and to sit for long periods. He has been compliant with his medication regimen. He has been wearing compression stockings but reports that they have not helped with the swelling. He received a steroid injection in his right knee last time he was here, which provided temporary relief. He has not had any relief since that time.\n\n1. Right knee pain and swelling.\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient is experiencing right knee pain and swelling, which has been present for the past 2 months. He has tried compression stockings with no relief.\n   - Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to try to reduce his salt intake, elevate his legs when possible, and to wear compression stockings. I also recommended that he follow up with his private care doctor if the swelling does not improve.\n   - Medical Treatment: We performed a fluid aspiration and steroid injection to the right knee today. We also ordered an x-ray of the right knee to check for any underlying causes of the swelling. We also ordered blood work for a complete metabolic panel to check kidney function.\n\n2. Edema in the legs.\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient has been experiencing left leg swelling.\n   - Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to try to reduce his salt intake, elevate his legs when possible, and to wear compression stockings. I also recommended that he follow up with his private care doctor if the swelling does not improve.\n   - Medical Treatment: We ordered an x-ray of the right knee to check for any underlying causes of the swelling.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me for the results of his blood work and x-ray. He will follow up with his pcp for the results of his x-ray and for further management of his rheumatoid arthritis. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee swelling and pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTyler Nelson is a pleasant 72-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for follow up of his right knee swelling and pain. The patient reports that his pain level is currently 3 out of 10, but typically it is around 6. The patient notes that his right knee has been swollen for the past 2 months. He states that the swelling has worsened over the past few weeks. He is unable to ambulate without the use of a cane. The patient states that the pain is worse when he first gets up from sitting. He has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily for the past 2 months.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nRheumatoid arthritis.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he loves salty snacks.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nXeljanz 11 mg daily\nCelebrex 200 mg daily\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Normal capillary refill in the toes. The right knee is warm to the touch. There is pitting edema around the right knee and ankle.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Swelling, erythema, and tenderness to palpation. Range of motion is slightly diminished. Effusion was attempted, but there was no fluid.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis.\n• Right knee swelling.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and medical history today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I explained that the right knee is swollen due to inflammation. I have recommended that we give him another steroid injection in the right knee. I have also recommended that we get an x-ray of the right knee to see where the swelling is coming from. The patient would like to have the injection today. I have also recommended that we set up physical therapy for him. I have explained that the swelling could be due to salt retention, so I advised him to reduce his salt intake. The patient states that he has already been wearing compression stockings. I advised him to continue to wear them and to elevate his legs at night. I have also recommended that he follow up with his primary care physician if his symptoms do not improve. I have ordered an x-ray of the right knee. The risks, benefits, and alternatives of the x-ray were explained. The risks, benefits, and alternatives of the steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nRight knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care physician. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen by me on 03/04/2021, where he received a steroid injection to his right knee. He continues to experience right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6/10. He notes when he stands after he has been sitting for a while, he has to use a cane to "get his bearings." The injection he received to the right knee at the last visit was only beneficial for a few weeks. The patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained