.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadrant abdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Anderson is a pleasant 64-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago. He describes the pain as squeezing and severe in nature. It is located on the right side of his abdomen, beneath his ribs. The pain is intermittent and is associated with nausea and vomiting. The patient states that his pain is related to his diet, particularly after consuming high-fat foods. He has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, which he controls with diet and exercise. The patient notes that he has been compliant with his diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. He reports that his symptoms have improved since he started a ketogenic diet. He also notes that he has a family history of gallbladder disease. His father and mother both had their gallbladder removed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fever. Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports nausea and vomiting. Denies hematemesis or melena.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n\nAbdominal\n- Inspection: No visible scars or bruising.\n- Palpation: Positive bowel sounds. Soft, nondistended. Slight guarding noted to the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness.\n- Percussion: Nontender to palpation in the right upper quadrant.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. There is some mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBobby Anderson is a 64-year-old male who presents with right upper quadrant abdominal pain. He has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and a family history of gallbladder disease. His abdominal ultrasound reveals multiple gallstones and mild inflammation of the gallbladder.\n\nCholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Given the patient\'s symptoms, physical exam findings, and imaging results, I believe the patient has mild cholecystitis with gallstones. He has not had any improvement with dietary modifications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that the best course of action would be to remove his gallbladder. I explained that this procedure is called a laparoscopic cholecystectomy and it will take about 1 to 2 hours. The patient will spend the night in the hospital for observation.\n- Medical Treatment: I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\n\nType 2 diabetes mellitus.\n- Medical Treatment: The patient will follow up with his primary care physician for management of his type 2 diabetes.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week for postoperative care. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Miller is a pleasant 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of his abdominal pain. The patient was seen by Dr. Miller in the office 3 weeks ago for the same complaint. The patient reports that he has been having pain on the right side of his abdomen, specifically underneath his ribs. He describes it as a squeezing pain that comes and goes. He rates his pain as an 8 out of 10 in severity. He states that it is associated with nausea. He denies any fevers or vomiting. The patient reports that he has a history of type 2 diabetes and was initially going to be managed with diet and exercise, but he has since started a keto diet which has resulted in a 25-pound weight loss over the past 3 months. The patient has a family history of gallbladder disease, both his mother and father required cholecystectomy.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain. Denies nausea and vomiting.\nGenitourinary: Denies hematuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies back pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, clicks, or murmurs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended. Positive bowel sounds. Slight guarding in the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild wall thickening is appreciated. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiologic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I explained to him that he has mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I explained that he has not had any improvement in his symptoms despite dietary modifications. I have recommended a laparoscopic cholecystectomy to remove his gallbladder. He will undergo the procedure in the next week. He will spend the night in the hospital for postoperative monitoring. He will need to avoid high fat foods after the surgery. I also discussed his type 2 diabetes with him today. I advised him to follow up with his primary care physician to ensure he is managing his diabetes well.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week after his surgery. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultras