ports that he had a right knee arthroplasty 2 years ago and has had good results with physical therapy since then.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain. Denies any pain to palpation of the lumbar spine.\n• Neurological: Denies any numbness or weakness in the legs.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Temperature: Afebrile\n• Blood Pressure: 117/56 mmHg\n• Heart Rate: 68 bpm\n• Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n• Oxygen Saturation: 95% on room air\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n• Examination: Bony protuberance of C5-C6.\n\nLumbar Spine\n• Examination: Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of paraspinal muscles.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n• Examination: Strength is 5/5 bilaterally in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine obtained and reviewed today are unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJeremy Allen is a 63-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of lower back pain. Based on the patient\'s history and physical examination findings, I believe the patient has an acute disk herniation.\n\n• Lumbar disk herniation.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that his symptoms began 4 months ago after moving boxes. He describes sharp pain with certain movements and tingling in his toes. His symptoms have not improved with conservative management.\n• Additional Testing: We will order an MRI of the lumbar spine to evaluate for a herniated disk.\n• Medical Treatment: I will refer the patient to physical therapy. I will also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that if he has a herniated disk, we may consider an epidural steroid injection to help reduce inflammation.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLower back pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Williams is a pleasant 53-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of lower back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago while moving boxes for his son who was moving out of his college dorm. The pain is located in the lower back and radiates into the right foot. He denies any numbness or tingling in his lower extremities at this time. The pain is worse with sitting and lifting. He denies any difficulty ambulating or voiding.\n\nALLERGIES\n\nNo known allergies.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Tylenol and ibuprofen to alleviate his symptoms.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports moving boxes for his son who was moving out of his college dorm, which resulted in the onset of his current symptoms.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports having a total knee replacement 2 years ago.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports having knee surgery 2 years ago.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain. Denies weakness in the legs. Denies numbness in the lower extremities.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals look good today. Blood pressure is 117/56, heart rate is 68, and oxygenation is 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No bony protuberance of the C5-C6.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination of the Lumbar Spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLumbar spine X-ray was obtained prior to the patient\'s visit today. It revealed no acute bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nHerniated disk.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we order a lumbar spine MRI to further evaluate his condition. I have also recommended that he continue with physical therapy. I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day