ay we will probably increase your dose of metformin from five hundred twice a day to seven fifty twice a day so why do n\'t we do that and then why do n\'t we have we\'ll have the hemoglobin a1c back and then we can once you get over this illness that you\'re undergoing we\'ll we\'ll we\'ll discuss that more i\'ll have you come back in about three weeks to just discuss your diabetes and what our treatment plan will be for that okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] for the high blood pressure definitely hold off on the norvasc for right now let\'s figure out what\'s going what\'s causing this episode of shortness of breath i want some blood testing done and and once we have that under better control we can reassess your blood pressure so right now hold the norvasc and then we\'ll have you come back once everything is done okay\n[patient] no\n[doctor] any other questions or anything we know if we need to talk about today\n[patient] no i\'m good\n[doctor] sounds good we\'ll get you signed out here in a second\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath with hemoptysis.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. He also has a long history of knee pain.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin and Norvasc.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nHENT: Denies bleeding gums.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath, hemoptysis.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports knee pain. Denies lower extremity edema.\nHematologic/Lymphatic: Denies bruising.\n\nVITALS\n\nPulse oxygenation: 94 percent. Blood pressure: 120/70 mm Hg.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Neck is supple. No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Some rales and rhonchi, more on the right. No wheezing right now. There is some diminished breath sounds in the right as well.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from before.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema on bilateral lower extremities. No calf tenderness. Negative Homan\'s sign.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: No bruising.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Shortness of breath.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been coughing up blood.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of further testing as his symptoms could be as simple as a pneumonia or something more serious.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered a chest x-ray, CT scan, and blood work to be done in the ER. I have instructed the patient to go into the ER immediately after this appointment and I will call ahead.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood sugar has not been well controlled.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered an updated A1c today. He will increase his metformin to 750 mg twice daily.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood pressure has been running low for approximately 3 weeks. I believe this could be caused by his shortness of breath. I would like to get a better understanding of the cause of his shortness of breath prior to pursuing further treatment for his hypertension.\n- Medical Treatment: At this time, he will discontinue the use of Norvasc.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe will have him follow up in about 3 weeks to just discuss his diabetes and further treatment recommendations.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he has been biking.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Norvasc and metformin.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath. Reports hemoptysis.\nCardiovascular: Denies leg swelling.\nGastrointestinal: Denies hematemesis or hematochezia.\nNeurological: Denies headaches or dizziness.\n\nVITALS\n\nPulse oximetry: 94% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Rales and rhonchi more on the right side. No wheezing. Diminished breath sounds in the right side.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 ejection murmur, unchanged from previous exam. Regular rate and rhythm otherwise.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Abdomen is soft.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ non-pitting edema in both lower extremities. No calf tenderness. Negative Homan\'s sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c: 7.0.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Shortness of breath.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with shortness of breath, hemoptysis, and a history of cough for the past 3 weeks. Given the hemoptysis, we need to rule out a possible pulmonary embolism or other pathology.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that he needs to get to the emergency department to get a chest X-ray and a CT scan of his chest. He will also need blood work done there to evaluate for a pulmonary embolism.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to the emergency department for further evaluation and treatment.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood sugar has been running high. The patient reports that his blood sugar is running high after meals and 150 in the morning.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that once he gets over this illness, we will increase his dose of metformin to 750 mg twice a day. We will also order another hemoglobin A1c today and follow up in 3 weeks to discuss his diabetes treatment plan.\n- Medical Treatment: Order hemoglobin A1c today. Increase metformin to 750 mg twice a day.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that his blood pressure has been running somewhat low, and he has been holding his Norvasc on occasion.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that we will hold the Norvasc until we get his pulmonary embolism ruled out.\n- Medical Treatment: Hold Norvasc.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Dennis Smith is a 57-year-old male who presents today with a chief complaint of shortness of breath. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma.\n\nMr. Smith reports that he has been experiencing shortness of breath for the past couple of weeks. He wakes up with shortness of breath and also experiences it when he tries to exert himself. He has also been coughing up blood, about 3 to 4 cups per day. He has not noticed any bruising or bleeding from his gums or teeth. He has been to the office, but was unable to get an appointment. He denies any fevers.\n\nMr. Smith has been experiencing intermittent knee pain for quite some time now. He denies any swelling. He denies any recent travel history.\n\nMr. Smith is on metformin 500 mg twice daily for his diabetes. He is also on Norvasc for his hypertension. He has a dexcom device that monitors his blood sugar levels. He reports that his blood sugar levels spike after meals, up to 200. He denies any low blood sugars. He states that his blood pressure has been running somewhat low, but he does not always take his medications.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fatigue.\nCardiovascular: Reports dyspnea on exertion.\nRespiratory: Reports dyspnea on exertion and hemoptysis.\nGastrointestinal: Denies hematochezia.\nGenitourinary: Denies hematuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Bilateral rales and rhonchi more on the right side. No wheezing. Diminished breath sounds on the right side.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 ejection murmur. Unchanged from previous exam. Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft and nontender.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema in both lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No Homan sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nOxygen level in the clinic today was 94% on pulse oximetry.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMr. Dennis Smith is a 57-year-old male who presents today with a chief complaint of shortness of breath. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma.\n\nShortness of breath.\n- Medical Reasoning: He has been experiencing shortness of breath for the past couple of weeks. He also reports coughing up blood. His oxygen level in the office today was 94% on pulse oximetry. On physical exam, he has rhonchi and diminished breath sounds on the right side.\n- Medical Treatment: We will refer him to the emergency department for a chest X-ray and a CT scan. He will also get some blood work done. Once he gets over this episode of shortness of breath, we will discuss his diabetes and treatment plan.\n\nDiabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood sugar levels have been running high, up to 200 after meals. He has a dexcom device that monitors his blood sugar levels. We will increase his dose of metformin from 500 mg twice daily to 750 mg twice daily.\n- Medical Treatment: We will increase his dose of metformin from 500 mg twice daily to 750 mg twice daily. We will repeat his hemoglobin A1C today.\n\nHypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: He has been experiencing intermittent low blood pressures. He is currently on Norvasc.\n- Medical Treatment: Hold the Norvasc for now. We will reassess his blood pressure once he gets over this episode of shortness of breath.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF C