ssures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Harris is a 45-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 4 days ago. She states that the pain radiates down her arm and along the medial aspect of her elbow. She denies any known trauma. The pain is constant and is exacerbated by use. She reports that her pain is approximately 7 out of 10. She states that she has difficulty sleeping due to the pain. She denies that she has injured her elbow before.\n\nThe patient reports that she has a history of hypertension and is currently taking 10 mg of lisinopril daily. She states that her blood pressures have been in the 130/80s. She also has a history of diabetes and is currently taking 500 mg of metformin twice a day. She admits to occasionally forgetting to take her medications.\n\nThe patient reports that she enjoys pottery and baking. She has been working on a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday and states that she is having difficulty working on it due to the pain. She also enjoys taking walks in the evening and in the morning.\n\nThe patient reports that she has tried taking ibuprofen for pain relief with no improvement.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain radiating down her arm and along the medial aspect of her elbow. Denies left elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 120/70.\nHeart rate: 78.\nRespiratory rate: 18.\nTemperature: 97.9.\nOxygen saturation: 100%.\nPulse: 78.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- No murmur, rub, or gallop.\n- Pulses are 2+ bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination of the right elbow: There is no bruising or discoloration. Tenderness noted on the medial aspect of the elbow. No erythema or warmth. No effusion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow is reviewed and is unremarkable. No fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that her x-ray did not reveal any fractures or bony abnormalities. I recommended rest, ice, and a sling for her right arm. She should apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. She should take 600 mg of ibuprofen three times a day for pain, for 1 week. She should not do any pottery for the next couple of weeks.\n- Medical Treatment: Ibuprofen 600 mg, 3 times a day for 1 week.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of diabetes and is currently taking 500 mg of metformin twice a day. She reports that she occasionally forgets to take her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: I recommended increasing her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. I advised her to check and record her blood sugars daily. I also recommended that she follow up with me in a couple of weeks to evaluate her blood sugars. I will order an A1c to evaluate her long-term blood sugar control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that it is important to take her medications as prescribed. I recommended that she keep a log of her blood sugars.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension and is currently taking 10 mg of lisinopril daily. She reports that her blood pressures have been in the 130/80s.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Lee is a pleasant 60-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of her pain started 4 days ago. The pain is located on the medial aspect of her elbow and radiates down her arm. She has been doing a lot of pottery and was creating a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She denies any direct injury. The pain is rated 7 out of 10 and interferes with her sleep. The patient has tried taking ibuprofen with minimal improvement. She denies any radicular symptoms.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension, which is controlled with lisinopril 10 mg daily. She reports that her blood pressures have been in the 130s-140s over the past few months. She states that she is compliant with her medications and has an alarm to remind her to take them. She also has a history of diabetes mellitus, which is controlled with metformin 500 mg twice daily. She admits to having a sweet tooth and has been consuming higher amounts of sugar lately. She also enjoys baking and making cakes and scones. She has been doing this since she was young. The patient states that she has had a diabetic consult in the past and is interested in speaking with a dietitian again.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nCardiovascular: Denies orthopnea.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain. Endorses dyspepsia.\nPsychiatric: Denies anxiety.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\nLungs: Clear bilaterally to auscultation.\n\nCardiovascular\nNo murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Equal pulses bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nNo bruising, swelling, or discoloration of the right elbow. Tenderness to palpation over the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and extension of the right wrist.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow is reviewed and reveals no bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n2. Diabetes mellitus.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nRight elbow pain.\n- Medical Reasoning:\n- Patient reports right elbow pain while making a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. X-ray of the right elbow reveals no bony abnormalities. This is consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n- Medical Treatment:\n- Obtain a sling to wear while awake. Apply ice to the right elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. Take 600 mg of ibuprofen, 3 times a day for 1 week. Hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of the injury and the treatment plan in detail. I advised the patient to rest the right elbow and avoid pottery for the next couple of weeks.\n\nDiabetes mellitus.\n- Medical Reasoning:\n- Patient reports that she is having difficulty with blood glucose control. She has a sweet tooth and enjoys baking. She is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment:\n- Increase metformin to 1000 mg, twice daily. Obtain an A1C. Follow-up in 2 weeks.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of monitoring her blood glucose levels and the importance of making lifestyle changes to better control her diabetes.\n\nHypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning:\n- Patient is compliant with her medications and has a good blood pressure control.\n- Medical Treatment:\n- Continue lisinopril 10 mg, daily.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining her current blood pressure control and the importance of lifestyle modifications to prevent the progression of her hypertension.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks for her diabetes mellitus. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n6. Section Relevance:\n    - Does the summary note accurately assign clinical information to the correct sections (e.g., patient-reported details in Subjective, objective findings in Objective, clinician insights in Assessment, and treatment strategies in Plan)?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
