y fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Miller is a pleasant 34-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his symptoms began approximately 2.5 weeks ago when he stepped off a curb. He denies that he fell down at the time of the injury. He notes that he initially experienced right ankle pain, but after a few days, he noticed that the pain shifted to his right knee. The patient states that his knee clicks when he walks, but there is no pain with walking unless he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. He states that he has been taking ibuprofen, but he is not a big pill taker and only uses it at night to help him sleep. He denies that his knee hurts when it is just resting on the floor. The patient reports that he is active and rides a bike for exercise. He enjoys peloton rides and states that he prefers to listen to rock music and regain music. The patient denies any numbness or tingling in his right foot, except for prior trauma to his second toe. He states that he has broken his second toe 3 times over the last 10 years. The patient denies that his current symptoms are new.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, clicking when walking, and numbness in the right foot.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right knee: Slight effusion. No pain to palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full range of motion. Negative Lachman sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee was obtained and reviewed today. This reveals no acute fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLateral collateral ligament strain of the right knee.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with him regarding his current symptoms. I have recommended that we treat him conservatively with meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I have also referred him to physical therapy. At this point, I do not believe that he will need to have an MRI. If he continues to have pain after physical therapy, we can consider further imaging. The patient states that he will be able to ice his right knee 20 on 20 off at least 4 times a day. He also plans to take ibuprofen as needed. The patient is employed as a sales representative and is currently working from home. He states that he has a high pain tolerance and does not find that his symptoms have worsened. The patient has been compliant with his treatment regimen and is willing to follow up as needed. He denies any questions or concerns at this time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me if his symptoms persist. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Rodriguez is a pleasant 49-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. He sustained an injury to his right knee 2.5 weeks ago when he stepped off a curb. He reports that he turned his ankle over to the outside and heard a pop. He denies any pain when he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. He denies any pain when he walks. He takes ibuprofen which helps with the pain at night. H