ss of breath, chest pain, or difficulty sleeping. He states he has been getting up and moving around without any problems. He has been monitoring his blood pressure at home and it has been good. The patient has been compliant with his medications since discharge.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills. Endocrine: Denies polyuria. Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain. Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath. Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting. Genitourinary: Denies dysuria. Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle pain. Neurological: Denies dizziness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/72. Heart rate: 79. Respiratory rate: 26. O2 sat: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck: No jugular venous distention.\nCardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nExtremities: Trace edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Acute on chronic heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms of ankle swelling and dyspnea on exertion are consistent with CHF. His echocardiogram shows a preserved EF with abnormal diastolic filling and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to monitor diet and avoid salty foods. Weigh daily and call if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days. Referral to a nutritionist for education on low-sodium diet.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure was elevated at 200/90 during his admission.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg daily. Renal artery ultrasound ordered to rule out secondary causes.\n\n3. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a known history of stage 3 CKD.\n- Medical Treatment: Blood work ordered to assess renal function.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees w