ss of breath, chest pain, or difficulty sleeping. He states he has been getting up and moving around without any problems. He has been monitoring his blood pressure at home and it has been good. The patient has been compliant with his medications since discharge.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills. Endocrine: Denies polyuria. Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain. Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath. Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting. Genitourinary: Denies dysuria. Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle pain. Neurological: Denies dizziness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/72. Heart rate: 79. Respiratory rate: 26. O2 sat: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck: No jugular venous distention.\nCardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nExtremities: Trace edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Acute on chronic heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms of ankle swelling and dyspnea on exertion are consistent with CHF. His echocardiogram shows a preserved EF with abnormal diastolic filling and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to monitor diet and avoid salty foods. Weigh daily and call if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days. Referral to a nutritionist for education on low-sodium diet.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure was elevated at 200/90 during his admission.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg daily. Renal artery ultrasound ordered to rule out secondary causes.\n\n3. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a known history of stage 3 CKD.\n- Medical Treatment: Blood work ordered to assess renal function.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute on chronic heart failure.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Johnson is a pleasant 82-year-old male who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic heart failure. He reports that he was doing well until he noticed that his ankles were swelling and he became short of breath. He called 911 and the paramedics placed him on a special mask. He was then evaluated at the Emergency Department where his blood pressure was noted to be 200/90 mmHg. He states that he has been trying to watch his diet as instructed. He has been taking his medications, but sometimes misses a dose or two. He is a big cheese lover and enjoys watching football and going to parties on the weekends. He loves pizza and chicken wings. He reports that he has been feeling well since his hospitalization. He has been urinating a lot and has been on a water pill through his IV. He has been taking his medications for his blood pressure as instructed. He has been checking his blood pressure at home and it has been good. He has been watching his diet and has not had any shortness of breath or chest pain. He has been sleeping well and has been taking an afternoon nap. He denies any chest pain. He reports that he has had a blood pressure cuff at home and has been checking his blood pressure every other day.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports taking Bumex 2 mg daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies weight changes.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGenitourinary: Denies hematuria.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure is 128/72 mmHg. Heart rate is 79 bpm. Respiratory rate is 26. Oxygen saturation is 99%. No jugular venous distention.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur which is stable.\n\nGenitourinary\n- Examination of the Lower Extremities: Trace edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMr. Johnson is a pleasant 82-year-old male who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic heart failure.\n\nAcute on chronic heart failure.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion in an uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to watch diet and avoid salty foods. He should avoid pizza and chicken wings. He should also avoid foods that are high in sodium. He should continue to check his blood pressure at home and contact me if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diet and weight control. He was given information about foods that are low in sodium.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is well controlled on current medications.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n• Medical Reasoning: We will order a renal artery ultrasound to be sure we are not missing another cause of hypertension.\n\nChronic Kidney Disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: We need to make sure he tolerates the regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: We will order more labs to check his kidney function.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up after acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports being a football fan.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Bumex 2 mg once daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTE