ks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess his progress and to determine his return to play date. He is advised to call the office should his symptoms change or worsen prior to his next appointment.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Smith is a pleasant 38-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right ankle pain. The onset of his pain began yesterday after he slipped on some ice while going out to take out the trash. The patient states he was very cautious but noticed a patch of ice. He was unable to ambulate on his right foot initially, but last night he was able to put a little bit more weight on it. He reports some limping. He states he has been keeping his foot elevated, icing it, taking ibuprofen, which has been helping.\n\nThe patient denies any numbness in his right foot. He states he has never injured this ankle before, considering he is very active and enjoys playing soccer.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports he is a soccer player and plays in a community league. He has a lot of sports injuries but does not recall injuring this ankle before. He enjoys playing soccer with his family. His wife is also into soccer. The patient’s children are starting to play soccer as well.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain and swelling.\nNeurological: Denies numbness in the right foot.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Heart sounds are normal, no murmurs.\nGastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft and non-tender.\nNeurological: Alert and oriented. Cranial nerves II-XII are grossly intact.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and