11 years old and again between 17 to 20 years old be screened for their cholesterol. We also discussed that studies link high cholesterol to pre-mature heart attacks. I advised the patient that genetic testing can be done to see if the problem is genetic or a secondary cause. They were also advised to follow a balanced diet with a variety of foods that are low in saturated fat and sugars, as well as remain active.\n- Medical Treatment: Full cholesterol panel ordered. Hemoglobin A1c, CMP, thyroid study, and liver panel were also ordered. Genetic testing ordered. Referral to nutritionist provided.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow-up in 3 to 6 months after meeting the nutritionist for another cholesterol level.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nElevated cholesterol.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary, immeasurably impressed minor who presents to the clinic for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia.\n\nThe patient reports that they were screened for cholesterol during an annual check-up and the result was elevated. They state that they have been active in the past year, riding their bicycle. They enjoy baking cookies but state that they try to eat a clean diet most days. The patient reports that they do not know of any family members with hypercholesterolemia.\n\nThe patient reports that they have a history of hypercholesterolemia. They state that they were seen by another provider last year and were told that their cholesterol was borderline high at that time. They deny any family history of heart disease or sudden death.\n\nThe patient reports that they used to live with parents who smoked but they no longer live with them. They state that they do not smoke and that no one they spends time with smokes.\n\nThe patient reports that they have been feeling well. They state that they have been meeting with a healthcare provider to discuss health goals and that they decided two years ago that they did not feel like a girl. They state that they have been living as non-binary since.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or shortness of breath.\n• Respiratory: Denies cough, wheezing, or shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle weakness.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or changes in vision.\n• Endocrine: Denies changes in appetite, weight, or temperature intolerance.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: Alert, oriented, and in no acute distress.\n• Heart: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n• Lungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Eyes: No xanthomas.\n• Neck: No thyromegaly.\n• Abdomen: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLipid panel: Total cholesterol 210 mg/dL.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJulie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary, immeasurably impressed minor who presents with elevated cholesterol.\n\n• Hypercholesterolemia, unspecified.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s cholesterol level was elevated at 210 mg/dL.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient on the importance of a well-balanced diet and regular exercise. I encouraged the patient to meet with a nutritionist.\n• Medical Treatment: I will order a full cholesterol panel, a complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels. Genetic testing will be performed to determine if this is a familial or secondary cause.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow-up in 3-6 months after meeting with a nutritionist and after dietary changes have been instituted. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nElevated cholesterol.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying bike riding and cooking. Patient is non-binary and prefers to be referred to as "they" or "them".\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports having a cousin who died at age 44 or 45. They believe the cousin had a heart attack.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fatigue.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nPsychiatric: Reports gender dysphoria.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure is normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\n- General Examination: In no apparent distress.\n- Musculoskeletal\n- Respiratory\n- Neck\n- Eyes\n- Ears, Nose, Mouth, and Throat\n- Cardiovascular\n- Gastrointestinal\n- Neurological\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Elevated cholesterol.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient was found to have elevated cholesterol on a screening test. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends that all children be screened once between the ages of 9 and 11 and again between the ages of 17 and 21.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the importance of maintaining a well balanced diet with the patient and encouraged them to continue with their exercise routine. I also discussed the importance of genetic testing.\n- Medical Treatment: We will order a full cholesterol panel, glucose, complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels. Genetic testing will also be performed to see if this is a familial problem or if there is a secondary cause.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow a well balanced diet including a variety of foods that are lower in saturated fat and sugars. They will meet with a nutritionist. They should continue to be active and exercise. We will follow up in 3 to 6 months after they have met with the nutritionist and instituted dietary changes. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHyperch