ports she works from home and tries to stay active. She enjoys running outdoors.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking ibuprofen and Aleve.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain, crepitus, and tenderness to palpation of the retinaculum.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals are normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\nMusculoskeletal: Normal gait, strength 3/5 for abduction of the legs bilaterally, remainder of muscle strength 5/5 in the lower extremities. Examination of the knees: No erythema or ecchymosis. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Pain with squatting. Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally. Neurological: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed and revealed no fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee pain, consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. We discussed that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. I recommended physical therapy to help strengthen the lower extremities and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I advised her to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain relief. I also advised her to take it easy from running for now, but we will continue to assess her progress with each visit.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she is an avid runner.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that she takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Gait: Normal gait.\n- Strength: 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for lower extremities are 5/5.\n- Examination of the Knee: No overlying erythema or ecchymosis appreciated. No warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Slight pain with squatting. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are all negative bilaterally.\n- Examination of the Elbow: No swelling, redness, or tenderness appreciated.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of both knees were reviewed and showed no fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Bilateral knee pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of the condition, as well as treatment options. I advised the patient to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain. We also discussed physical therapy and how it will help increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics.\n- Medical Treatment: I will be referring the patient to physical therapy. She will continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain control.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with pain. Follow up with me in a few weeks to assess her progress. She should avoid running for now. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She deni