NT ILLNESS\n\nKirk Smith is a pleasant 28-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began approximately 2 weeks ago when he was lifting a box of books. He describes the pain as achy and sharp in nature and localized to his low back. The pain initially did not radiate but is now starting to radiate down his left leg. The patient denies any numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. He also denies any weakness. He states that he has tried over-the-counter ibuprofen and Tylenol with occasional relief. This is the first time he has discussed the option of surgery. He reports that his activity level is moderate, he exercises a few times a week, but is inconsistent. He has a sedentary job. He denies smoking. He has a history of playing soccer in the past.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain, radiating down the left leg.\nNeurological: Denies numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. Denies weakness.\nGeneral: Denies loss of bladder or bowel control.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 130/70.\nRespiratory rate: 18.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the back is positive for midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space. Right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion and extension. Positive straight leg test.\nNeurological: Decreased sensation to touch to the left leg. Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray and MRI of the lumbar spine was performed prior to the patient\'s arrival today. The MRI shows a disc desiccation, disc bulge with paracentral disc herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLumbar strain at L4-L5 due to a herniated disc with radiculopathy.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to his current symptoms. I have explained that his symptoms are in line with a herniated disc at L4-L5 with nerve impingement. I recommended a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and a corticosteroid, which I will inject into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy. The patient is in agreement with this plan. All questions were answered to the patient\'s satisfaction.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week for reassessment. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKyle Davis is a pleasant 32-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 2 weeks ago when he was lifting a box of books. He denies using proper lifting technique. At first, he did not have any pain, but it has progressively worsened. He describes the pain as "achy and sharp" in his low back. It radiates down his left leg. The pain is not worse with any specific position. He denies any numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, or weakness. He denies any loss of control of his bladder or bowels. He has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol, which sometimes helps. He denies any previous discussions about surgery. The patient is an Olympic weightlifter and has a sedentary job. He tries to exercise a few times a week. He has a history of playing soccer. He denies smoking. He has a father with back pain. The patient reports he is a big fan of the Tatu Hotsper.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 130/70\nRR: 18\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Examination of the spine: Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Positive straight leg raising bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray and MRI of the lumbar spine were reviewed today. The MRI demonstrates disk desiccation, disk bulge, with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLumbar back pain at the L4-L5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we proceed with a lumbar epidural injection with a strong non-steroidal anti-inflammatory (NSAID) and corticosteroid into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy. This should help with the inflammation that is causing his pain. We can schedule this today if he would like.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week for a follow-up visit. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKyle Morales is a pleasant 58-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain.\n\nOnset of his pain began 2 weeks ago, when he was lifting a box of books and felt a pop. The pain is described as aching and sharp. His pain has been increasing since the time of onset and is beginning to radiate down his left leg. The patient denies any pain with sitting. He adds that he has a sedentary job. The patient states that his pain does not affect his job, however he feels that he does not want to be as active as he was previously. He tries to exercise a few times per week, however he is inconsistent. He denies any numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, weakness, and loss of control of his bladder or bowels.\n\nThe patient has been taking ibuprofen and Tylenol for pain, which provides some relief. He has not been evaluated for surgery.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nHe denies smoking. The patient has a history of playing soccer in the midfield position.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nThe patient states that his father has back pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\nNeurological: Denies bowl or bladder control issues, numbness, tingling, decreased sensations, or weakness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 130/70 mm Hg.\nRespiratory rate: 18.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: I do not appreciate any murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort no respiratory distress. Clear to auscultation, bilaterally.\nBACK: No evidence of trauma or deformity\nNEURO: Normal sensation. Patellar and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\nMSK: Examination of the lumbar spine: No ecchymosis noted. Midline tenderness at the L4-5 disc space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Positive supine straight leg test.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThe MRI of the lumbar spine was reviewed today. It revealed disc desiccation and a disc bulge with a paracentral disc herniation resulting in moderate right neural foraminal stenosis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLumbar back pain at the L4-5 disc space due to a herniated disc with radiculopathy.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and MRI findings today, I have discussed with the patient that his MRI revealed a herniated disc with radiculopathy. We discussed treatment options for this and I have recommended that we begin with conservative treatment in the form of a corticosteroid injection. With the patient\'s consent, we will proceed with a corticosteroid injection into the lumbar spine today. The patient was made aware of the risks of bleeding, infection, nerve damage, blood vessel damage, reaction to the medication, including skin changes, swelling, and also, the risk of elevated glucose levels if the patient were diabetic.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary n