atient states she is unable to bear weight on her left leg.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the left knee: There is edema present. There is effusion. There is moderate range of motion. There is pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s test. Negative varus and valgus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee were obtained and reviewed today. There are no fractures or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have explained to the patient the nature of her diagnosis. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. I have recommended that we start the patient on a pain medication that is not an NSAID. I have prescribed the patient Tramadol 50 mg, oral, twice a day, as needed for pain. She can also take Tylenol for breakthrough pain. I have recommended that we place the patient in a knee immobilizer for the next week to keep the knee from moving and to reduce some of the inflammation that she is seeing. I have recommended that she ice her knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have also advised her that she should not run for the next 3 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks if she is not improving. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAngela Rodriguez is a pleasant 56-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. She states that she sustained this injury one week ago while playing with her niece. She was running around with her niece who is learning how to ride a bike without training wheels and she fell and twisted her knee. The patient reports that she heard a pop at the time of injury. She states that she is currentl