 reports that she has not had any recent seizures. She reports that her last seizure was a year ago. She reports that she is still taking Keppra.\n\nRegarding her depression, she reports that she started therapy last year and she is trying to avoid medications for this problem. She reports that therapy has been helpful, but she still feels down and stressed. She denies suicidal and homicidal ideations.\n\nRegarding her peptic ulcer, she reports that she was dealing with a lot of stress last year. She then underwent an endoscopy which showed that she had an episode of gastritis. She was subsequently diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease and started Protonix. She reports that she has not had any issues with her ulcer since the procedure and everything has been good.\n\nThe patient denies chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting. She reports that she does have a little bit of medial nasal congestion from allergies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Ears, Nose, Mouth and Throat: Endorses medial nasal congestion from allergies.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow joint pain, swelling, and weakness.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow reveals erythema and edema. Pain to palpation of the left olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLeft arm x-ray does not reveal any evidence of elbow fracture.\n\nEGD revealed an episode of gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAnna Morris is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with some joint pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: I believe she has left olecranon bursitis caused by repetitive trauma.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate meloxicam 15 mg once daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nJoint pain, particularly in the left elbow.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nAnna Nguyen is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease, who presents with moderate joint pain in her left elbow. The patient reports that the pain has been ongoing for several months and is exacerbated by her job as a software engineer, which involves prolonged sitting and typing. She experiences daily stiffness and swelling at the elbow, making it difficult to open and close her left hand. She is right-handed but has been able to write with her left hand. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands or weakness in her arm.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Endorses swelling and erythema of the left elbow.\n- **Neurological:** Denies numbness or tingling in hands, weakness in the arm.\n- **General:** Denies fever or chills.\n- **Psychiatric:** Endorses mild depressive symptoms, currently in therapy, previously diagnosed depression.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** History of peptic ulcer disease, currently managed with protonix.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n- Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Oxygen Saturation: 98% on room air\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert and oriented, well-nourished, no acute distress.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Erythema and edema of the left elbow, pain with palpation of the olecranon, warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.\n- **Neurological:** No focal deficits observed.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- **Left Arm X-ray:** No evidence of elbow fracture.\n- **Endoscopy:** Showed an episode of gastritis, consistent with patient\'s history of peptic ulcer disease.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n1. **Left elbow pain (ICD-10 M25.521):**\n   - Olecranon bursitis, likely secondary to repetitive trauma from her job.\n2. **Epilepsy:**\n   - Well-managed with current medication.\n3. **Depression:**\n   - Mild depressive symptoms, managed with therapy and potential future medical intervention.\n4. **Peptic Ulcer Disease:**\n   - History of episodes, currently managed with protonix.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n1. **Left elbow pain:**\n   - **Medical Reasoning:** Likely olecranon bursitis.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Discussed the use of a pillow to support the elbow during work hours and the importance of taking breaks to avoid further strain.\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Prescribed melo- meloxicam 15 mg, oral, once daily, 30 tablets, 1 refill.\n   - **Additional Testing:** Referral to a physical therapist for a tailored exercise regimen.\n   - **Follow-up:** Follow-up in 2 weeks.\n\n2. **Epilepsy:**\n   - **Medical Reasoning:** Well-managed with current medication.\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Continue keppra 500 mg, oral, twice daily, 90 tablets, refill.\n\n3. **Depression:**\n   - **Medical Reasoning:** Mild depressive symptoms, previously diagnosed.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Discussed the benefits of continuing therapy and potential medical treatment options.\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Start lexapro 10 mg, oral, once daily, 30 tablets, 1 refill.\n\n4. **Peptic Ulcer Disease:**\n   - **Medical Reasoning:** History of episodes, currently managed.\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Continue protonix 40 mg, oral, once daily before meals, 30 tablets.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Dax is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with left elbow pain. Dax works as a data entry clerk at a local hospital. She sits at a desk all day typing on a keyboard. She states that she repetitively flexes her left elbow to the extent of 90 degrees, specifically at the keyboard and mouse. She states that after work, it is difficult for her to extend and flex her left elbow. She reports left elbow pain, swelling, and stiffness. She denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in her left arm. She denies any fever, chills, chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting. She endorses nasal congestion from allergies.\nDax is right-hand dominant. She can still write but has difficulty typing. She has not had any recent seizures. She is currently taking keppra 500 mg twice daily for epilepsy. She has been in therapy for depression and has not wanted to take any medication since she is already on keppra. She has been experiencing some stressors in her life. She has had an episode of gastritis a year ago that required an upper endoscopy, which showed peptic ulcer disease. She has been taking protonix 40 mg once daily before meals and has not had any further issues with her peptic ulcer disease.\nOn physical examination, she has vital signs within normal limits. She has erythema and edema of her left elbow. She has pain to palpation of the olecranon. She is warm to palpation. She has some weakness in her left arm. She can barely flex her left elbow. She has a palpable right radial pulse. She has a normal left radial pulse. She has no other focal neurological deficits.\nHer left elbow x-ray is shown below in Figure. Her endoscopy is shown below in Figure.\nBased on the information, Dax has olecranon bursitis from repetitive trauma, meloxicam 15 mg once daily, ice the area, and a pillow for her elbow. She is doing well with her epilepsy and will continue keppra 500 mg twice daily. She is doing well with her depression and will start lexapro 10 mg once daily. She will continue protonix 40 mg once daily before meals for her peptic ulcer disease. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with some joint pain.\n\nThe patient reports that her left elbow has really been bothering her. She states that she sits at her desk all day for work with her elbows bent. She reports that after work it is really hard for her to flex and extend her left elbow. She reports that it has been really stiff and painful. She also notes that she has noticed swelling on the olecranon of her elbow. She reports that she is right-handed, therefore, she can still write, which is great, but typing is difficult. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She reports that she has some weakness in her arm but nothing too significant. She denies any fever or chills.\n\nRegarding her epilepsy, she reports that she has not had any recent seizures. She reports that her last seizure was a year ago. She reports that she is still taking Keppra.\n\nRegarding her depression, she reports that she started therapy last year and she is trying to avoid medications for this problem. She reports that therapy has been helpful, but she still feels down and stressed. She denies suicidal and homicidal ideations.\n\nRegarding her peptic ulcer, she reports that she was dealing