 history of chronic back pain that has been intermittent, but right now it has been doing well. She denies pain radiating down her legs.\n\nThe patient has a history of depression, which has been going really well with medication and therapy. She is trying to get outside more and it has been helping her with her depression. She notes she is sleeping and eating well. She denies concerns about hurting herself or others.\n\nThe patient\'s carpal tunnel is doing really well. She has been getting full range of motion and it has felt a lot better.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses right knee joint pain. Endorses chronic back pain.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neurological/Psychological: Appropriate mood and affect. Grips are equal bilaterally.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n• Musculoskeletal: No lower extremity edema. Pain with patellar inhibition. Pain with range of motion of the right knee.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee is unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMs. Thompson is a 27 y.o. female who presents today for evaluation of right knee pain.\n\nChondromalacia of right patella.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been more active lately but is unaware of any injury to the knee. Her recent x-rays were normal but she did have some discomfort on exam.\n• Medical Treatment: Prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food. I do want her to do some straight leg raising exercises that will strengthen her quadriceps.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged her to participate in physical activity that is less straining on her joints , such as swimming freestyle laps, or to stop activity all together. If her pain does not improve in a couple of weeks, she will follow up.\n\nChronic back pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is stable and well-controlled at this time.\n• Medical Treatment: She should continue swimming regularly once her knee is feeling better.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her current regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current medications.\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome.\n• Medical Reasoning: Relatively stable status post carpal tunnel release.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen with no changes at this time.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nKnee pain during swimming.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nRebecca, a 27-year-old female, presents with moderate pain in her right knee during and after swimming. The pain has been present for the past week and has increased over the course of the month. She describes the pain as starting after swimming a few laps, particularly with the breaststroke, and has noticed a reduction in her ability to perform this stroke. She denies any injury or trauma to the knee.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports moderate pain in the right knee during and after swimming, with a reduction in ability to perform breaststroke. No other joint pain or swelling noted.\n- Neurological: No radicular symptoms.\n- General: Good sleep, adequate diet, no concerns regarding suicidal or homicidal ideation.\n- Psychiatric: History of depression, currently on medication, reports good compliance and participation in therapy, and increased outdoor activities.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- **Musculoskeletal:**\n  - Right knee: Moderate tenderness with palpation around the patella, limited range of motion with pain on patellar inhibition test. No swelling observed in other joints.\n- **General:**\n  - Well-appearing, no acute distress.\n- **Cardiovascular:**\n  - Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\n- **Respiratory:**\n  - Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n- **Neurological:**\n  - Alert and oriented, cranial nerves II-XII intact.\n- **Endocrine:**\n  - No thyromegaly or lymphadenopathy.\n- **Psychiatric:**\n  - Mood and affect appropriate, no suicidal or homicidal ideation.\n\n**Results:**\n- **X-ray of the right knee:** Normal x-ray, no abnormalities detected.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n1. **Chondromalacia of Patella, Right Knee**\n   - Medical Reasoning: Patient presents with moderate pain during and after swimming, with a reduction in ability to perform breaststroke. Physical exam findings of tenderness around the patella and pain with patellar inhibition suggest chondromalacia of the patella.\n   \n2. **Chronic Back Pain**\n   - Medical Reasoning: Chronic back pain, previously diagnosed, currently well-managed.\n\n3. **Depression**\n   - Medical Reasoning: Patient reports stable depression, currently on medication, with good compliance and participation in therapy.\n\n4. **Carpal Tunnel Syndrome (resolved)**\n   - Medical Reasoning: History of carpal tunnel release surgery, currently well.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n1. **Chondromalacia of Patella, Right Knee**\n   - Medical Treatment: \n     - Ibuprofen 600 mg, oral, 4 times a day with food, 30 tablets.\n   - Physical Therapy:\n     - Recommend straight leg raising exercises to strengthen quadriceps.\n   - Patient Education and Counseling:\n     - Advised to avoid activities that exacerbate knee pain for the next 2 weeks.\n     - Encourage continuation of swimming but modify techniques to reduce stress on the knee.\n   - Follow-up Recommendations:\n     - Follow-up appointment in 2 weeks if no improvement.\n\n2. **Chronic Back Pain**\n   - Continue current management and lifestyle modifications as previously advised.\n\n3