ear-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with a positive long-term Lyme titer. She reports moderate severity of symptoms including fatigue, lack of energy, and swollen joints in the elbows, which have persisted for the past 2 weeks. The patient notes that she recently had an insect bite while hiking in wooded areas, which she suspects may have led to the Lyme disease. She reports a bull\'s-eye rash that has since resolved. She has been experiencing a decreased appetite and mild nausea, which have impacted her ability to perform daily activities.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** Reports fatigue and lack of energy.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports swollen joints in the elbows.\n- **Integumentary:** Reports a bull\'s-eye rash on the abdomen.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Reports mild nausea and decreased appetite.\n- **Endocrine:** Reports stable blood sugars but has had occasional highs and lows.\n- **General:** Denies fever or chills.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Patient appears fatigued but in no acute distress.\n- **Vital Signs:** No fever noted.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Swelling of the elbow joints bilaterally with pain to palpation.\n- **Skin:** Bull\'s-eye rash on the left abdomen, warm to palpation, non-tender to palpation.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\n**Laboratory Results:**\n- **Lyme Titer:** Elevated\n- **CBC:** Normal white blood cell count\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** Lyme Disease, confirmed by elevated Lyme titer.\n- **Secondary Diagnoses:**\n  - Type 2 Diabetes, well-managed on metformin.\n  - Hypertension, well-managed on lisinopril.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Lyme Disease:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** Elevated Lyme titer confirms diagnosis of Lyme disease.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Prescribe doxycycline 100mg BID for 3 weeks.\n  - **Patient Edu