*\nMichelle Johnson is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with a positive long-term Lyme titer. She reports moderate severity of symptoms including fatigue, lack of energy, and swollen joints in the elbows, which have persisted for the past 2 weeks. The patient notes that she recently had an insect bite while hiking in wooded areas, which she suspects may have led to the Lyme disease. She reports a bull\'s-eye rash that has since resolved. She has been experiencing a decreased appetite and mild nausea, which have impacted her ability to perform daily activities.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** Reports fatigue and lack of energy.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports swollen joints in the elbows.\n- **Integumentary:** Reports a bull\'s-eye rash on the abdomen.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Reports mild nausea and decreased appetite.\n- **Endocrine:** Reports stable blood sugars but has had occasional highs and lows.\n- **General:** Denies fever or chills.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Patient appears fatigued but in no acute distress.\n- **Vital Signs:** No fever noted.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Swelling of the elbow joints bilaterally with pain to palpation.\n- **Skin:** Bull\'s-eye rash on the left abdomen, warm to palpation, non-tender to palpation.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\n**Laboratory Results:**\n- **Lyme Titer:** Elevated\n- **CBC:** Normal white blood cell count\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** Lyme Disease, confirmed by elevated Lyme titer.\n- **Secondary Diagnoses:**\n  - Type 2 Diabetes, well-managed on metformin.\n  - Hypertension, well-managed on lisinopril.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Lyme Disease:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** Elevated Lyme titer confirms diagnosis of Lyme disease.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Prescribe doxycycline 100mg BID for 3 weeks.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Educate the patient on the importance of completing the full course of antibiotics and monitoring for any worsening symptoms.\n  - **Referral:** Referral to an infectious disease specialist, Dr. Emily Carter, for further management of Lyme disease.\n  - **Follow-up:** Follow-up appointment in 1 week.\n\n- **Type 2 Diabetes:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** Blood sugars appear stable but monitoring is recommended.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Order hemoglobin A1c.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Reinforce the importance of blood sugar monitoring and adherence to diet and medication regimen.\n\n- **Hypertension:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** Blood pressure is currently stable.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Continue lisinopril 10mg daily.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Encourage continued monitoring of blood pressure at home and adherence to dietary recommendations.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n**INSTRUCTIONS:**\nThe patient will follow up in 1 week for re-evaluation of symptoms and lab results. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ We are going to go right to the front-. so when you touch that- hi, michelle, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m good. how are you?\n[doctor] i\'m good. so i know the nurse told you about dax. i\'d like to dax a little bit about you, okay?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] all right. so, michelle is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with abnormal lab finding. so, michelle, it came back to me that you had had a positive long... lyme titer.\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] and so, uh, can you, you, you know, can you tell me a little bit about it? i know the last time i saw you, you had had an insect bite, and you know, it came back positive for lyme. so how are you feeling?\n[patient] i\'ve been feeling... i could feel better. um... i\'ve been feeling