ch she thought would help build muscle and strengthen her knees. However, she has found that these activities have actually increased her joint pain. She denies any other associated symptoms such as tingling or swelling in her feet.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports moderate joint pain in both knees, difficulty with stair climbing, and prolonged standing. No other joint pain reported.\n- **Neurological:** Denies tingling or numbness in extremities.\n- **Endocrine:** History of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, reports no recent changes in blood sugar levels but notes frequent monitoring due to diabetes.\n- **General:** Denies fever or chills.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Reports history of hypertension, currently managed with medication. Denies chest pain or palpitations.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Denies nausea or vomiting.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: Elevated at 145/90 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert and oriented, appears well-nourished.\n- **Neck:** No thyromegaly, neck supple.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n- **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Examination of the left knee shows slight erythema and edema, with a small effusion present. Decreased flexion and extension of the knee. Strength 5/5 in lower extremities.\n- **Neurological:** No focal deficits, normal gait.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- **X-ray of the left knee:** Normal, slight strain noted.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** Other specified joint pain (M25.89)\n- **Secondary Diagnoses:**\n  - Hypertension, currently managed with Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n  - Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, currently on Metformin 500 mg BID.\n  - History of rotator cuff repair 2 years ago.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Medications:**\n  - Meloxicam 15 mg, oral, once daily, 30 tablets.\n- **Physical Therapy:**\n  - Referral for physical therapy to address joint pain and improve mobility.\n- **Blood Work:**\n  - Autoimmune profile (ANA, Rheumatoid factor, ESR, CRP).\n- **Imaging:**\n  - MRI of the left knee to evaluate for any underlying structural issues.\n- **Follow-up Recommendations:**\n  - Follow-up appointment in 4 weeks for reassessment.\n- **Lifestyle Modifications:**\n  - Continue regular exercise but modify activities to avoid aggravating joint pain.\n  - Maintain a balanced diet with reduced carbohydrate intake.\n- **Hypertension Management:**\n  - Monitor blood pressure at home and report any elevated readings.\n- **Diabetes Management:**\n  - Continue current medication regimen (Metformin 500 mg BID).\n  - Monitor blood glucose levels regularly.\n\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Janet is a 33-year-old female who comes in for some, some joint paint. What, what joint\'s been bothering you, Janet?\n[Patient] It\'s been mostly, um, my knees, actually. I\'ve been doing more stair type workouts, which I thought was, you know, building muscle, uh, on my knees and supposed to help.\n[Doctor] Mm-hmm.\n[Patient] But in turn it, it really hasn\'t, so my knees have been really bothering me.\n[Doctor] Okay. All right. And is it one knee versus the other? Or both equally?\n[Patient] It\'s both equally.\n[Doctor] Okay. And have you been having any other joint pain like, elbows or shoulder, or anything like that?\n[Patient] No, but I, I should mention that like, I had a rotator cuff repair about two years ago.\n[Doctor] Mm-hmm.\n[Patient] Um, but I haven\'t had any problems since that point.\n[Doctor] Okay, and what shoulder was that?\n[Doctor] That was my right shoulder.\n[Doctor] Okay, all right. And, um, any pins and needles in your feet at all? Any swelling in your legs?\n