d several cups of coffee on an empty stomach the morning of. He denies additional episodes of palpitations.\n\nHe has been monitoring his blood pressure at home daily, obtaining systolic blood pressure readings of 146 to 155. He does report a family history of hypertension. Additionally, he has been monitoring his salt intake and feels he is doing well at reducing it. He also walks on his treadmill as often as he can.\n\nHe has a new primary care physician who started him on a new medication Benicar HCT, which he has been using for the past month.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nSeveral family members positive for hypertension.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nBenicar HCT 40 mg/25 mg\nAmlodipine 10 mg daily.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nCardiovascular\nNormal rate, regular rhythm, S2 normal, normal heart sounds, and normal pulses.\n\nPulmonary/Chest\nBreath sounds normal. \n\nAbdominal\nThere is no tenderness.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nThere is no edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThyroid profile 03/01/2021.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Resistant hypertension.\n\nMr. Roberts presents with resistant hypertension, with systolic blood pressure readings between 146-155. He is currently on maximal doses for 3 medications.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have added spironolactone to his regimen which will require monitoring of his potassium and sodium. We will schedule him for a follow up in 2 weeks to complete a basic metabolic panel and check his blood pressure. Next medication to consider adding is carvedilol. I have tried to avoid this, given the potential for issues with erectile dysfunction. I counseled the patient about spironolactone side effects, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances, as well as his increased risk of dehydration, with one sign to watch for being dizziness. If we are not able to improve his blood pressure with spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to the resistant hypertension clinic.\n\nI provided and reviewed with Mr. Roberts the American Heart association website (heart.org) and encouraged him to access for tips on lowering his salt intake and increasing his potassium intake. His maximum salt intake should be 1500 to <2000 mg per day. He was provided materials to follow the DASH diet and encouraged to meet 30-min of walking most days.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSchedule follow up appointment in 2 weeks for blood pressure check and basic metabolic panel. Reduce sodium intake to 1500 to <2000 mg per day. Follow DASH diet.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nFollow-up from recent ER visit for high blood pressure and palpitations.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nMr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old African American male with a history of hypertension and palpitations. He reports that his blood pressure was elevated during a recent emergency room visit. He notes that he was previously on amlodipine 10 mg daily but was switched to benicar hct (40-25 mg) one month ago by his new primary care physician. He denies any chest pain or shortness of breath. He believes his symptoms were exacerbated by high caffeine intake on the day of the ER visit and not having breakfast. He has not experienced any recent episodes since the visit.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Cardiovascular: Reports palpitations during the ER visit, no chest pain.\n- Respiratory: No shortness of breath.\n- Gastrointestinal: No nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports walking on the treadmill occasionally.\n- Neurological: No dizziness, headaches, or syncope.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 146/95 mmHg (home monitoring).\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm.\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\n- Temperature: 98.6°F.\n\n**General Physical Exam:**\n- General: Alert, oriented, no acute distress.\n- Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n- Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- Extremities: No edema.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- Blood Work: \n  - Metabolic Panel (awaiting results).\n  - Thyroid Profile (awaiting results from March 1st, 2021).\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Hypertension, Unspecified (I10)**\n  - Patient presents with resistant hypertension despite being on three medications (amlodipine 10 mg daily and benicar hct 40-25 mg daily).\n  - Blood pressure remains elevated at 146/95 mmHg with a home monitoring reading.\n  - Previous thyroid profile ordered (awaiting results from March 1st, 2021).\n  - Elevated potassium at 4.0 mmol/L (awaiting repeat test).\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Medication Adjustment:**\n  - Start spironolactone 25 mg, oral, once daily. Dispense 30 tablets.\n  \n- **Follow-up Recommendations:**\n  - Schedule follow-up in 2 weeks to check blood pressure and electrolytes.\n  \n- **Referral:**\n  - Referral to Dr. Emily Carter, a cardiologist, for resistant hypertension management.\n\n- **Patient Education:**\n  - Discussed the potential side effects of spironolactone, including electrolyte imbalances and gynecomastia.\n  - Advised on the importance of adhering to a low-salt diet and increasing potassium intake.\n  - Discussed the potential need for further intervention if blood pressure is not controlled.\n\n- **Lifestyle Modifications:**\n  - Encouraged routine exercise, such as walking on the treadmill for 30 minutes most days.\n  - Advised reducing salt intake and increasing potassium-rich foods.\n  - Recommended monitoring blood pressure at home and keeping a log.\n\n- **Patient\'s Understanding:**\n  - Patient is cooperative and willing to follow medical advice.\n  - Provided information on the DASH eating plan and resources for dietary counseling.\n\n- **Next Steps:**\n  - Monitor blood pressure and electrolytes in 2 weeks.\n  - If not controlled, consider referral to the resistant hypertension clinic for further management and potential intervention.\n\n**Note:**\n- Patient has a history of hypertension and palpitations. Fa