 an odor in her vaginal region. She says that the right side of her vagina is starting to become painful but denies any fever or chills. She states that she recently learned her partner of 3 years has been engaging in sexual encounters with other people for quite some time, and she wants to be evaluated for all sexually transmitted diseases.\n\nMs. Ross reports noticing a change in the odor, color, and consistency of her vaginal discharge last week. She notes that this did not alarm her until the right side of her vagina became painful and sore, which has not subsided. She states that she had an abnormal Pap Smear around the age of 20, but all her Pap Smears since then, including her most recent one approximately 6 months ago, have all been normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGenitourinary\nPelvic exam: No external lesions, normal labia majora and labia minora. Normal physiological discharge with normal color and odor. Cervix is normal, vaginal wall is normal, no cervical motion tenderness or masses noted. Swabs were obtained for sexual transmitted disease testing.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Screening for sexually transmitted disease.\n\nPLAN\n\nMs. Ross is a 23-year-old female who presents today as a new patient for full-spectrum STD testing. We will screen her today for gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomoniasis, syphilis, HIV, and hepatitis C. Patient is high risk due to an abnormal pap smear in the past and the recent discovery of her sexual partner having multiple partners for some period of time. We will call the patient with the results of the laboratory testing.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nPatient presents for a full spectrum STD testing. Reports symptoms of discomfort, change in vaginal discharge, and odor. She mentions that the right side of her vagina is starting to become painful. She denies any fevers or chills.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nMs. Ross is a 22-year-old African American female who is a college student and part-time waitress. She is here for a preventive health screening for STDs. She has a history of an irregular pap smear at age 20. She reports that she recently discovered her sexual partner has been involved in sexual encounters with other people. She is anxious about the possibility of having an STD and wants to be evaluated comprehensively.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **General:** No fevers or chills.\n- **Genitourinary:** Reports change in vaginal discharge, odor, and mild pain on the right side of the vagina.\n- **Psychological:** Anxious about potential STDs.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 118/76 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert, cooperative, and in no acute distress.\n- **Pelvic Exam:**\n  - External: No lesions noted.\n  - Labia majora and minora: Normal.\n  - Vaginal wall: Normal.\n  - Cervix: Normal.\n  - Speculum: Normal physiological discharge, normal color and smell.\n  - No cervical motion tenderness.\n  - No adnexal tenderness or masses.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Diagnosis:** Encounter for screening for sexually transmitted diseases (ICD-10 Z13.1).\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **STD Testing:**\n  - Swabs for Gonorrhea, Chlamydia, Trichomoniasis.\n  - Blood for syphilis, HIV, and Hepatitis C.\n- **Follow-Up:**\n  - Patient will be called if results are positive.\n  - Patient advised to return in 3 days or sooner if symptoms worsen.\n- **Lifestyle Recommendations:**\n  - Use condoms consistently.\n  - Limit the number of sexual partners.\n  - Consider regular STD screenings.\n- **Follow-Up Visit:**\n  - Scheduled follow-up visit in 3 days for results.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- Ms. Ross is advised to return in 3 days or sooner if symptoms worsen. She is also advised to call the office if she has any questions or concerns about her test results. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{