ctive:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Pulse Rate: 68 bpm\n- Temperature: Afebrile\n- Oxygen Saturation: 99% on room air\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- **Abdominal:** Soft, nondistended, positive bowel sounds. Mild guarding noted in the right upper quadrant. Positive for Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness or peritoneal signs.\n- **Neurological:** Alert and oriented, no focal deficits.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- **Abdominal Ultrasound:** Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder, mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Common bile duct size within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Diagnosis:** Calculus of gallbladder with acute and chronic cholecystitis without obstruction.\n- **Medical Reasoning:** The patient presents with classic symptoms of cholecystitis, including right upper quadrant pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. The ultrasound findings confirm the presence of gallstones and mild gallbladder wall thickening, consistent with chronic cholecystitis.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Surgical Treatment:** Laparoscopic cholecystectomy is planned and scheduled.\n- **Patient Education and Counseling:** Discussed the procedure, expected outcomes, and postoperative care. Advised the patient to avoid high-fat foods post-surgery and to follow a diet high in fiber.\n- **Follow-Up:** Postoperative follow-up is scheduled for 1 week to assess recovery and wound healing.\n- **Additional Recommendations:** Continue monitoring and management of type 2 diabetes mellitus. Follow-up with the endocrinologist for diabetes management as outlined in the current treatment plan.\n\nThe patient understands and agrees with the proposed treatment plan and follow-up schedule. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Bobby is a sixty six year old male who presented to Dr. Mi