pine exam, there was no tenderness to palpation of the cervical spine. There was full range of motion on head exam. There did not appear to be any trauma. There was no ecchymosis or bruising on the right shoulder exam. On his musculoskeletal exam, there was pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. There was decreased abduction and adduction to about ninety degrees only. There was there is a he has pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. There was a palpable radial radial artery pulse.\nThe nurse did a shoulder x-ray on the patient before the doctor came in the room. The right shoulder x-ray showed that the patient has an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. The doctor came in and told the patient that he has a a a small fracture of the acromion. The doctor stated that it it\'s gon na be nothing that we need to do surgery for. The doctor stated that he wants to go ahead and just put the patient in a sling for now. The doctor stated that he wants to go ahead and prescribe meloxicam fifteen milligrams once a day. The doctor stated that he wants to go ahead and just order a cat scan of the right shoulder just to kinda get a better look of how the joint looks. The doctor stated that he\'s going to go ahead and refer the patient to physical therapy to strengthen the shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight shoulder injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMatthew Allen is a 67-year-old male, right-hand-dominant, male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right shoulder injury. The patient sustained this injury 4 days ago, when he was tackled a couple of times playing pick up football and landed on his right shoulder. He states that he was able to return to the game, however he has had pain since that time. The patient locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his shoulder. He denies any numbness or tingling in his fingers. He s