tor] it might make you feel better but we\'ll we\'ll deal with that when we get get there okay\n[patient] sure\n[doctor] any questions\n[patient] not at this point\n[doctor] okay great thank you\n[patient] thank you\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Adams is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago, when he was moving boxes out of his son’s college dorm. At that time, he noted tingling in his right toes, which resolved after approximately 1 hour. After his injury, he felt as though he was unable to support his upper body weight. The patient reports weakness in his legs when carrying heavy objects. He reports a sharp pain in his lower back when he turns a certain way or rolls over in his sleep. The pain will resolve with repositioning. The patient was seen at urgent care and was referred to obtain an MRI, however he did not undergo this secondary to insurance issues. He has been utilizing Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice for pain, which does not provide relief. The patient notes the ibuprofen is slightly more beneficial than the Tylenol, however it does not relieve his symptoms. He denies upset stomach with the use of ibuprofen. Additionally, he attempted physical therapy at his gym without any relief. He denies any bladder or bowels issues, fevers, and chills.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of a right total knee arthroplasty 2 years ago, in spring of 2020. He states that his right knee feels 95% improved since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGastrointestinal: Denies bowel issues.\nGenitourinary: Denies bladder issues.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: Normal.\nBlood pressure: 117/56 mm Hg.\nHeart rate: 68 BPM.\nOxygenation: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Bony protuberance of the C5-C6. No pain to palpation of the C5-C6\nMSK: Examination of the Lumbar spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were reviewed. These reveal no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLow back pain, possible acute disc herniation.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. His x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture. I would like to obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to evaluate for a possible acute disc herniation. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. He will continue with physical therapy and a referral was provided for this today.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nPatient presents with moderate to severe lower back pain that has persisted for 4 months.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nJeremy Thompson is a 58-year-old male who presents today for an initial consultation regarding lower back pain. The pain began 4 months ago following a recent incident where he felt a sudden "twang" in his lower back while moving boxes for his son who was moving out of his college dorm. The pain is described as moderate to severe and is associated with numbness in the toes of his right foot. The numbness is intermittent but occurs periodically with prolonged sitting or short periods of movement, causing significant difficulty in performing daily tasks such as sitting for extended periods or carrying heavy objects. He reports that the pain is relieved by certain positions but wakes him up from sleep if he turns a certain way.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** Denies fever, chills.\n- **Neurological:** Endorses numbness in toes of the right foot, denies weakness in the lower extremities.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Endorses moderate to severe lower back pain, relieved by certain positions, difficulty with prolonged sitting and carrying heavy objects.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Temperature: Afebrile\n- Blood Pressure: 117/56 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 68 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Oxygen Saturation: 95% on room air\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- **Neck:** Bony protuberance of C5-C6.\n- **Lumbar Spine:** No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine, decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, positive straight leg raise, spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, positive straight leg raise, spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Lower extremities strength is equal bilaterally.\n\n**Results:**\n- Lumbar Spine X-ray: No acute bony abnormality; no fracture detected.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\nJeremy Thompson is a 58-year-old male with a past medical history of a total knee replacement in the right knee (performed 2 years ago) who presents with moderate to severe lower back pain and numbness in the toes of the right foot. The pain has persisted for 4 months following a recent incident. The physical exam reveals decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, a positive straight leg raise, and spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. The lumbar spine X-ray shows no acute bony abnormality.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Acute Disk Herniation, Site Unspecified\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Medical Reasoning:**\n- The patient\'s symptoms and physical exam findings are consistent with a diagnosis of acute disk herniation. Given the duration of symptoms and the severity, further imaging is warranted to confirm the diagnosis and assess the extent.\n\n**Patient Education and Counseling:**\n- Discussed the nature of the diagnosis and the importance of adhering to physical therapy. Explained the potential need for further interventions based on the MRI results.\n\n**Medical Treatment:**\n- Continue with physical therapy.\n- Prescribed meloxicam 15 mg, oral, once daily, 30 tablets.\n\n**Additional Testing:**\n- Order a lumbar spine MRI to further evaluate the extent of the disk herniation.\n\n**Referrals:**\n- Referral to a physical therapist through the health system.\n- Referral to Dr. Emily Carter, Orthopedic Specialist, for further evaluation and management of the potential disk herniation.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\n- The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- Follow-up appointment scheduled in 4 weeks to review MRI results and assess progress with physical therapy.\n##### }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Jeremy is a 67-year-old male who was moving boxes for his son who was moving out of his college dorm when he injured his back. He presented to the clinic four months after his injury. He had tried physical therapy at his gym and scheduled an appointment with me. He reported that he had pain with sitting, pain with movement for a very short period of time, and it felt like he was going to “tapple” over, he could not support his my upper body weight, and his right toe or the the toes on his right foot tingled a little bit but that went away after about an hour. He stated that the sensation has gone and he has not had any tingling or numbness since. He only had difficulty or weakness when he was carrying something heavy otherwise it seemed relatively normal but he felt the sensation so he was a little nervous about carrying something heavy. He also reported that if I turned a certain way or if I rolled over in his sleep he would wake up from it from a like a sharp pain but then that that went away if he if he positioned himself the right way. He took some tylenol, he took some ibuprofen, he’s tried heat, he’s tried ice and nothing really seems to help. He stated that the ibuprofen was a little bit better than the tylenol for sure a shorter period of time but he tries not to take too much so he i don\'t have an upset stomach from it. He went to an urgent care and they wanted him to do an mri but he was a little concerned because he thought we maxed out our insurance so he he didn\'t do it because it started to feel better but because it’s been going on so long he’s he’s a little bit concerned now he he does wan na actually move forward with that. He denied any incontinence, any problems with his bladder or his bowels, any difficulty with defecation, any difficulty with urination, any other symptoms. He had a knee surgery about two years ago on his right knee, that went fine, he rehabbed it pt has been fine it it feels about ninety five percent ever since. He had a total knee replacement in twenty eighteen, so it was actually about two years ago so it’s the spring of twenty twenty. On his physical exam, he had vital signs were good, he had no temperature, his blood pressure was good, it was about one seventeen over fifty six, his heart rate was nice and slow at sixty eight and his oxygenation was fine at ninety five percent on room air. On his neck exam, I i do appreciate some bony protuberance of the c5 c6. He had no pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. There was decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. The patient does have a positive straight leg raise and there is some spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine on musculoskeletal exam. The lower extremities strength was equal bilaterally. The patient had lumbar spine x-ray done before he saw me and I looked at the results of his lumbar spine x-ray which showed no acute bony abnormality. He had no fracture of his back or anything which is not surprising based on his history. So I do believe it sounds like he has an lumbar spine mri. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Adams is a pleasant 57-year-old male 