ss in toes of the right foot, denies weakness in the lower extremities.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Endorses moderate to severe lower back pain, relieved by certain positions, difficulty with prolonged sitting and carrying heavy objects.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Temperature: Afebrile\n- Blood Pressure: 117/56 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 68 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Oxygen Saturation: 95% on room air\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- **Neck:** Bony protuberance of C5-C6.\n- **Lumbar Spine:** No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine, decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, positive straight leg raise, spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, positive straight leg raise, spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Lower extremities strength is equal bilaterally.\n\n**Results:**\n- Lumbar Spine X-ray: No acute bony abnormality; no fracture detected.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\nJeremy Thompson is a 58-year-old male with a past medical history of a total knee replacement in the right knee (performed 2 years ago) who presents with moderate to severe lower back pain and numbness in the toes of the right foot. The pain has persisted for 4 months following a recent incident. The physical exam reveals decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, a positive straight leg raise, and spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. The lumbar spine X-ray shows no acute bony abnormality.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Acute Disk Herniation, Site Unspecified\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Medical Reasoning:**\n- The patient\'s symptoms and physical exam findings are consistent with a diagnosis of acute disk herniation. Given the duration of symptoms and the severity, further imaging is warranted to confirm the diagnosis and assess the extent.\n\n**Patient Education and Counseling:**\n- Discussed the nature of the diagnosis and the importance of adhering to physical therapy. E