een coughing up blood for the past 3 weeks, producing 3-4 cups of blood per day. He denies any hemoptysis, leg swelling, or recent travel history. He reports difficulty walking long distances due to shortness of breath and has had trouble getting an appointment in the office due to being "mostly numb."\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** Reports poor sleep quality, waking up short of breath.\n- **Respiratory:** Reports hemoptysis and moderate shortness of breath.\n- **Cardiovascular:** No chest pain or palpitations.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** No nausea or vomiting.\n- **Neurological:** No headaches or dizziness.\n- **Endocrine:** Reports blood sugars have been high and low, currently on metformin.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports knee pain, no leg swelling.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 80 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: Afebrile\n- Pulse Oxygenation: 94% on pulse oximetry in the clinic\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert, in mild respiratory distress.\n- **HEENT:** No JVD, no carotid bruits.\n- **Respiratory:** Rales and rhonchi heard more on the right side, diminished breath sounds in the right lung field.\n- **Cardiovascular:** 2/1 holosystolic ejection murmur, unchanged from previous exams, regular rate and rhythm.\n- **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, no organomegaly.\n- **Extremities:** 1+ non-pitting edema in both lower extremities, no calf tenderness, negative Homan\'s sign.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n1. **Shortness of breath with hemoptysis:**\n   - Differential Diagnosis: Possible pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, or malignancy.\n   - Awaiting results from emergency department evaluation and chest CT scan.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes:**\n   - Hemoglobin A1c: 7.0%\n   - Blood sugars have been high post-meals and low in the morning.\n\n3. **Hypertension:**\n   - Blood pressure within normal limits but history of hypertension.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n1. **Shor