is:**\n   - Referral to Emergency Department for further evaluation and imaging.\n   - Order a Chest CT scan to rule out pulmonary embolism or other pathology.\n   - Blood work to be done at the emergency department including CBC, BMP, D-dimer, and ABG.\n   - Follow-up with pulmonologist, Dr. Sarah Thompson, for further evaluation and management.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes:**\n   - Order Hemoglobin A1c today.\n   - Increase metformin dose to 750 mg BID.\n   - Schedule follow-up in 3 weeks for diabetes management and review of hemoglobin A1c results.\n\n3. **Hypertension:**\n   - Hold current antihypertensive medication (Norvasc) temporarily until further evaluation for the current episode of shortness of breath.\n   - Reassess blood pressure and hypertension management after the current episode is resolved.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- The patient is advised to visit the emergency department immediately for further evaluation and to undergo the recommended tests.\n- The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Dennis is a 57 -year-old male who presented to the office today complaining of shortness of breath. He also had a history of diabetes, high blood pressure, and history of asthma. Dennis told me that he was not feeling well for the last couple of weeks. He said he wakes up with shortness of breath. He goes to when he is short of breath there he gets back home and he is again short of breath so he had a tough time dealing with this. He said he has noticed that he has been i’ve been coughing up i’ve been coughing up blood. He said this has been going on for about three weeks or so. He said he has been coughing three to four cups a day. He said he has been coughing up blood only when he coughs. He said there is no bruising or bleeding from his teeth or from his gums or anything like that. He said there is no leg swelling or calf swelling or any any anything like that. He said he had some knee problem bu