 ecchymosis let me just feel around here scott how does that feel does that hurt when i kinda press on that there\n[patient] a little bit of pain there not not really bad it\'s it feels like it\'s just more on the inside of my knee\n[doctor] yep okay so you definitely feeling some a little bit of pain to palpation at the medial joint line now i\'m gon na have you lie on your back and also i\'m gon na do some maneuvering here of your leg i\'m gon na do a real quick test on you here okay alright how does that feel\n[patient] just feels just feels loose it hurts when you pull it at the very end\n[doctor] okay sorry about that alright so you\'re definitely having some difficulty with some passive range of motion here and you\'re lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to about a hundred and fifteen degrees as well as lacking about maybe twenty to thirty degrees of terminal flexion on the secondary secondary to the pain here and you do have a positive lachman exam so one more thing i wan na take a look at real quick i\'m gon na take a look at your toes here real quick very good so neurovascularly your bilateral lower extremities are intact and it looks like you have a muscle strength of a five out of five so scott you know based on my examination here your right knee injury is really suggestive of anterior cruciate ligament tear and you\'ve heard of it probably even heard as acl tear sometimes especially when a lot of people do sports and they injure their knee that that can happen so my plan for you though is i wan na i wan na go ahead and order an mri because i wan na evaluate the integrity of that acl now you may need surgery but we\'ll we\'ll first take a look at that mri result and reevaluate and reevaluate that first but in the meantime i wan na go ahead and order some anti-inflammatory medication meloxicam fifty milligrams a day it\'s gon na help reduce that swelling and the pain and then i want you to go ahead and ice the knee if you need to if you\'re still seeing some swelling and some pain you can go ahead and use ice as well but i definitely would like for you to moderate your activities so i would like for you to wear a knee immobilizer and then also use crutches when you walk around just to help you a little bit so not to put too much stress and strain on that knee does that sound like a plan\n[patient] it it does now well i need to limit my work activities i i typically sit behind a desk but it\'s not been very comfortable sitting here lately\n[doctor] yeah definitely so yeah so you you can limit the activities for sure in terms of your work activities i know if you sit too long sometimes it can be painful because you do need to move that leg a little bit so it does n\'t lock up so i i would like for you to you know do do some slight activities not enough to stress your legs but definitely move move them a little bit every now and then so it does n\'t lock up on you okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright i do want you to i know you are i know you actually jog often so i want you to just slow that down for a bit no jogging for a while until we get you know results of your mri till we figure out what we what we are gon na be doing okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright and how\'s your dog you has it been jogging with you before this incident\n[patient] how\'s just how\'s my dog or how is my job\n[doctor] your your dog\n[patient] my my my dog is okay\n[doctor] good\n[patient] is doing really well and and he enjoys still playing around outside but looks like wo n\'t be able to do that for a little bit\n[doctor] very good alright well i\'m gon na go ahead and have the nurse take you to the scheduler to get your mri and i\'ll get back with you as soon as we get the results of that\n[patient] okay sounds good thank you\n[doctor] thank you\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion; however, when he goes to straighten his knee, he feels like he has to help it straight. The patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee, however it feels loose and does not feel normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works at a desk.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Marked limp in the right leg upon ambulation.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Difficulty with passive ROM. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. Lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain. Positive Lachman\'s test. Muscle strength is 5/5. Neurovascular intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, possible anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. We discussed that the patient should utilize ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. I recommended that he wears a knee immobilizer and use crutches when he is ambulating. He will avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nRight knee pain and limited function.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nScott Johnson is a 29-year-old Caucasian male who presents for an initial consultation after experiencing right knee pain and swelling for the past 3 days. The patient reports that the injury occurred while playing in a recreational soccer game. He describes the pain as moderate, rated between 6-7/10, and notes swelling that has reduced slightly. The patient mentions limited range of motion and difficulty bearing weight on the affected leg. He has been managing the pain with anti-inflammatory medications such as ibuprofen, which provides some relief. The pain is persistent and has impacted his ability to participate in recreational sports and daily activities, including jogging, which he does regularly.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, limited range of motion, and difficulty bearing weight.\n- General: No other systemic symptoms reported.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **Gait:** Marked limp observed on the right leg upon ambulation.\n- **Knee Examination:** \n  - Significant effusion in the right knee.\n  - Slight ecchymosis.\n  - Pain to palpation at the medial joint line.\n  - Difficulty with passive range of motion.\n  - Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension (to 115°).\n  - Lacking 20-30° of terminal flexion secondary to pain.\n  - Positive Lachman exam.\n\n**Neurovascular Exam:**\n- Neurovascularly intact in bilateral lower extremities.\n- Muscle strength 5/5 in bilateral lower extremities.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Right knee injury, likely ACL tear.\n- Differential Diagnosis: ACL tear, meniscal injury, ligamentous injury.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Imaging:** Ordered MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL.\n- **Medication:** Prescribed anti-inflammatory medication (Meloxicam 50 mg, oral, once daily, 30 tablets).\n- **Activity Modification:** Advised to wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches to aid in ambulation.\n- **Follow-up:** Follow-up appointment in 2 weeks to review MRI results and re-evaluate the need for surgical intervention.\n- **Lifestyle Change:** Advised to moderate physical activities, limit jogging, and avoid high-impact sports until further notice.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- The patient should continue to use the knee immobilizer and crutches as needed.\n- Apply ice to the knee if swelling persists.\n- Take the prescribed medication as directed and monitor for any side effects.\n- Avoid strenuous activities that may exacerbate the knee pain.\n- Return for a follow-up visit in 2 weeks to discuss MRI results and further management. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ A 31-year-old male, Scott, came into the office complaining of a right knee injury. He stated that he had been playing soccer over the weekend and went to go plank kick the ball and felt a pop in his right knee. He stated that he had been active and had been jogging often. He also stated that he has a dog that he enjoys playing around with. He noticed some swelling, but some of that swelling had gone down. He stated that his knee is just not functioning as well as it used to be. It seems to be a little limited. He stated that the pain is ranging from a six to a seven on a scale of one to ten, one being the least pain and ten being the worst pain. He stated that the pain is about a four or five with some of the swelling going down. He took anti-inflammatories and stated that the ibuprofen helped a little bit but he is on it pretty on a pretty regular basis. He stated that he can bend it but it hurts towards the end of the motion and he can straighten it but it doesn\'t it feels like he has to help get it straightened. He stated that he can still bear weight on it but it feels just really loose it it doesn\'t feel normal. He had a marked limp on his looks like it\'s his right leg upon ambulation. Upon physical exam, we noticed a significant effusion in the right knee just a slight bit of ecchymosis. He felt some pain to palpation at the medial joint line. He felt some difficulty with passive range of motion. He lacked a few degrees of terminal extension to about a hundred and fifteen degrees as well as lacked about maybe twenty to thirty degrees of terminal flexion on the secondary to the pain. He had a positive lachman exam. We also evaluated the integrity of his toes and he had a muscle strength of a five out of five. Neurovascularly his bilateral lower extremities were intact. The right knee injury was really suggestive of anterior cruciate ligament tear. The plan was to go ahead and order an mri because we wanted to evaluate the integrity of that acl. He was told that he may need surgery but we would first take a look at that mri result and reevaluate and reevaluate that first. He was also told to go ahead and take anti-inflammatory medication, meloxicam fifty milligrams a day, and it\'s gon na help reduce that swelling and the pain. He was also told to ice the knee if he needed to if he was still seeing some swelling and some pain. He was told to moderate his activities so he would wear a knee immobilizer and then also use crutches when he would walk around just to help him a little bit so not to put too much stress and strain on that knee. He was also told that he would need to limit his work activities because he sits behind a desk but it\'s not been very comfortable sitting here lately. He was also told to do slight activities not enough to stress his legs but definitely move move them a little bit every now and then so it doesn\'t lock up on him. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion; however, when he goes to straighten his knee, he feels like he has to help it straight. The patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee, however it feels loose and does not feel normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works at a desk.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Marked limp in the right leg upon ambulation.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Difficulty with passive ROM. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. Lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain. Positive Lachman\'s test. Muscle strength is 5/5. Neurovascular intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, possible anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. We discussed that the patient should utilize ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. I recommended that he wears a knee immobilizer and use crutches when he is ambulating. He will avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n6. Section Relevance:\n    - Does the summary note accurately assign clinical information to the correct sections (e.g., patient-reported details in Subjective, objective findings in Objective, clinician insights in Assessment, and treatment strategies in Plan)?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


