the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nRight knee pain and limited function.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nScott Johnson is a 29-year-old Caucasian male who presents for an initial consultation after experiencing right knee pain and swelling for the past 3 days. The patient reports that the injury occurred while playing in a recreational soccer game. He describes the pain as moderate, rated between 6-7/10, and notes swelling that has reduced slightly. The patient mentions limited range of motion and difficulty bearing weight on the affected leg. He has been managing the pain with anti-inflammatory medications such as ibuprofen, which provides some relief. The pain is persistent and has impacted his ability to participate in recreational sports and daily activities, including jogging, which he does regularly.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, limited range of motion, and difficulty bearing weight.\n- General: No other systemic symptoms reported.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **Gait:** Marked limp observed on the right leg upon ambulation.\n- **Knee Examination:** \n  - Significant effusion in the right knee.\n  - Slight ecchymosis.\n  - Pain to palpation at the medial joint line.\n  - Difficulty with passive range of motion.\n  - Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension (to 115°).\n  - Lacking 20-30° of terminal flexion secondary to pain.\n  - Positive Lachman exam.\n\n**Neurovascular Exam:**\n- Neurovascularly intact in bilateral lower extremities.\n- Muscle strength 5/5 in bilateral lower extremities.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Right knee injury, likely ACL tear.\n- Differential Diagnosis: ACL tear, meniscal injury, ligamentous injury.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Imaging:** Ordered MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL.\n- **Medication:** Prescribed anti-inflammatory medication (Meloxicam 50 mg, oral, once daily, 30 tablets).\n- **Activity Modification:** Advised to wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches to aid in ambulation.\n- **Follow-up:** Follow-up appointment in 2 weeks to review MRI results and re-evaluate the need for surgical intervention.\n- **Lifestyle Change:** Advised to moderate physical activities, limit jogging, and avoid high-impact sports until further notice.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- The patient should continue to use the knee immobilizer and crutches as needed.\n- Apply ice to the knee if swelling persists.\n- Take the prescribed medication as directed and monitor for any side effects.\n- Avoid strenuous activities that may exacerbate the knee pain.\n- Return for a follow-up visit in 2 weeks to discuss MRI results and further management. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ A 31-year-old male, Scott, came into the office complaining of a right knee injury. He stated that he had been playing soccer over the weekend and went to go plank kick the ball and felt a pop in his right knee. He stated that he had been active and had been jogging often. He also stated that he has a dog that he enjoys playing around with. He noticed some swelling, but some of that swelling had gone down. He stated that his knee is just not functioning as well as it used to be. It seems to be a little limited. He stated that the pain is ranging from a six to a seven on a scale of one to ten, one being the least pain and ten being the worst pain. He stated that the pain is about a four or five with some of the swelling going down. He took anti-inflammatories and stated that the ibuprofen helped a little bit but he is on it pretty on a pretty regular basis. He stated that he can bend i