hese reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nRight knee pain and clicking sensation.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nGregory Davis is a 30-year-old African American male who presents to the urgent care center with complaints of moderate pain and a clicking sensation in his right knee, which he noticed after an incident 2.5 weeks ago. The patient reports that he heard a "pop" when he stepped off a curb. Since then, he has experienced intermittent clicking and occasional locking of the knee, particularly when walking, but no pain during prolonged standing. He denies any significant impact on daily activities except for difficulty with full squats. The patient has not taken any medication for the pain, opting to manage it through activity and rest. He reports that ibuprofen helps with nighttime sleep but has minimal adherence to medication overall.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Musculoskeletal: Positive for moderate pain and clicking sensation in the right knee, difficulty with full squats.\n- Neurological: Positive for numbness and tingling in the foot, likely due to a previous foot fracture.\n- General: No fever or chills.\n- Cardiovascular: No chest pain or palpitations.\n- Respiratory: No shortness of breath or cough.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- General: Well-nourished, well-hydrated, appears in no acute distress.\n- Musculoskeletal:\n  - Right knee: Slight edema appreciated, no pain to palpation of the right lateral knee, full range of motion with no instability, positive varus test, negative Lachman sign.\n- Cardiovascular: Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\n- Neurological: No focal deficits.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- X-ray of the right knee: No acute fracture or bony abnormality.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- Sprain of the lateral collateral ligament of the right knee.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- Prescribed meloxicam 15 mg orally once daily for pain management.\n- Advised physical therapy to strengthen the surrounding muscles and improve knee stability.\n- Recommended icing the knee 20 minutes on, 20 minutes off, 4 times a day.\n- Instructed to avoid high-impact activities such as burpees and to use supportive footwear.\n- Follow-up in 4 weeks for re-evaluation.\n- Referral to physical therapy for targeted rehabilitation exercises.\n- MRI of the right knee if symptoms persist post-physical therapy to evaluate for any ligament damage or other structural issues.\n\nThe patient was cooperative and expressed understanding of the treatment plan. He has a high health literacy and is motivated to recover quickly to resume his active lifestyle, including regular cycling and Peloton workouts. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Gregory is a 49-year-old male who presented to the office after injuring his right knee stepping off a curb. He reported that he was not running, but rather he turned over his ankle to the outside and thought his ankle was the thing that was going to hurt most, but after the ankle soreness went away the only his knee hurt and it sort of clicked when he would walk. It didn\'t hurt when he would walk, unless he stood on one foot for an extended period of time, like leaning his foot up against the wall. He would feel like it was locked and then he would sort of have to ease into walking again. He took ibuprofen but wasn\'t a big pill taker, so he sort of sucked it up and didn\'t really take it seriously. At night it did help him to settle down and sleep better. It didn\'t hurt when he was just resting and sitting there, when it was up it was fine when it was down on the floor with his foot flat it was fine it did hurt when he rested it on his heel. He was able to do things like bending, things like that. Squats were when he felt the most clicking like if he went all the way down to a full squat like a catcher that\'s when he felt the most clicking but otherwise no there was no pain it was just the clicking and he was fearful that that was an injury of some type. He was active, he rode the bike, he rode the bike which he wasn\'t doing now peloton inside for the winter he did have a winter bike though. He had more favorite rides, he liked rock music and regain music rides so whoever was doing those in a began or a medium content he was cool with. He couldn\'t picture himself as a regade person so that was interesting to know. He did have some numbing and tingling but it wasn\'t new, he had broken his foot his second toe on his right foot and he had numbness depending upon the shoe he wore. He had broken it three times over the last ten years so it was just something that was a an annoyance but it wasn\'t caused by this injury.\nOn physical exam, the patient had a slight effusion, there was no pain to palpation of the right lateral knee, there was a positive varus test, there was full range of motion, there was a negative lachman sign, the patient did on cardiovascular exam the patient did have a palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse. The patient had an x-ray of the knee done in the office and the results of the knee x-ray were reviewed by the doctor and it does not show any acute fracture or bony abnormality which is not surprising based on his injury. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Price is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 2.5 weeks ago, when he stepped off of a curb and turned over his ankle. He locates his pain to the lateral aspect of his right knee. The patient also reports clicking with ambulation. He denies any pain with ambulation unless he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. The patient also reports locking of his knee with prolonged standing. He notices pain when he is resting his foot on his heel. He denies any pain at rest and elevated, or when his foot is flat on the ground. The patient states that he is more active during the day, which does not impact his knee pain. He adds that he is able to perform a full squat without pain. The patient reports numbness and tingling in his right foot, however this is not new. The patient has been taking ibuprofen, which provides him with relief at night. He has also been icing his knee 4 times per day, which provides him with relief in the morning and at the end of the day.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nHe states that he has fractured his 2nd toe 3 times over the last 10 years.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works in sales. He rides a bike for exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nNeurological: Positive for numbness and tingling in his toes on his right foot from prior injury.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: >Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Slight edema. Slight effusion. No pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full ROM. Negative Lachman\'s.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the right knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other 