intake. Prescription for Zantac twice daily provided. I\'m going to order a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and a comprehensive metabolic panel.\n\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n- Medical Reasoning: She is doing well, but I want to make a slight adjustment to her dosage.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue with home monitoring and bring those readings to her next appointment.\n- Medical Treatment: Metformin 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg at night.\nContinue home blood sugar monitoring.\n\n3. Asthma.\n- Medical Reasoning: She seems to be doing well on her current regimen and has not had any asthma attacks lately.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: She was advised to take Flovent daily despite no recent asthma attacks as this will decrease the frequency of her attacks.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue on daily Flovent. Continue albuterol as-needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nAbdominal pain.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nBarbara Lee is a 31-year-old female with a history of diabetes mellitus type 2 and asthma who presents with a one-week history of moderate abdominal pain. She reports that the pain is gradually increasing in severity and is associated with nausea and occasional vomiting. The nausea has been intermittent, occurring 3 times in the past week. She notes that her pain is exacerbated by physical activity and has impacted her ability to exercise, including her usual jogging routine. She has a longstanding history of coffee consumption, including frequent visits to Starbucks, and has recently increased her intake. She is highly motivated to follow medical advice and has been cooperative with previous treatments.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** No fever.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Moderate abdominal pain, nausea, occasional vomiting.\n- **Endocrine:** Blood glucose levels within controlled range, no hypo/hyperglycemic episodes reported.\n- **Respiratory:** No recent asthma attacks, uses flovent Budesonide 200 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert and oriented, no acute distress.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n- **Abdominal:** Mild tenderness in the epigastrium, no rebound tenderness, no guarding. Abdomen soft, no tenderness in the right lower quadrant, no significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n- **Respiratory:** Mild expiratory wheezes, otherwise normal lung exam, excellent air movement.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n1. **Acute Gastritis, unspecified, without bleeding.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms of abdominal pain, nausea, and occasional vomiting, along with her recent increase in coffee intake and history of alcohol use, are consistent with acute gastritis. Physical exam findings support this diagnosis.\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood glucose levels are within the controlled range, and she is currently on metformin 500 mg once daily, which was increased to 500 mg twice daily in the previous visit.\n3. **Asthma.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s asthma is currently well-controlled on flovent 200 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed. No recent exacerbations reported.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n1. **Acute Gastritis.**\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Prescribed Omeprazole 20 mg, oral, twice a day, 30 tablets.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Advised the patient to reduce caffeine and alcohol intake. Recommended cutting back on spicy food as well.\n   - **Follow-up:** Follow-up in 4 weeks.\n   - **Additional Testing:** Ordered urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and CMP to rule out other possible causes of abdominal pain.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.**\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Continue metformin 500 mg in the morning and 500 mg in the evening. Monitor blood glucose levels.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Continue dietary modifications to manage blood glucose levels.\n\n3. **Asthma.**\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Continue flovent 200 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Encouraged the patient to continue her current asthma management plan.\n\n**Patient Agreements:** The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n##### }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Barbara is a 31 -year-old female with a history of diabetes type two and asthma who presented with abdominal pain. She reported that the abdominal pain had been present for one week and was gradually increasing in severity. Along with the abdominal pain, she also had nausea and vomiting on and off. She stated that she had experienced nausea for approximately three times in the past week. She also vomited a couple of times. She admitted to having a history of alcohol abuse and also a history of coffee intake. She reported that she had been drinking a lot of coffee lately, and she thought that it could have triggered her gastritis. She also reported that she had been exercising a lot lately and that her abdominal pain would increase when she worked out. She stated that she would like to jog or go for a run and her abdominal pain would just “spike” and she would have to take it easy. She also reported that she had not had any fever with that pain. She had a longstanding history of diabetes mellitus type two and was taking metformin 500 milligrams once a day. She was also on flovent 50 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed for wheezing. She had not had any asthma attacks lately and thought she could decrease her flovent use. She reported that she had been drinking a lot of coffee, and she thought that it could have triggered her gastritis.\nOn physical exam, her heart exam was unremarkable with the exception of a grade three out of six systolic ejection murmur that was unchanged from her prior exam. She was not in any acute distress. She had a soft abdomen with mild tenderness in the epigastrium of her abdomen. Her abdomen was otherwise soft with no tenderness in the right lower quadrant. There was no significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant. She had mild end expiratory wheezes very faint and otherwise normal lung exam with excellent air movement. Her skin was warm and dry, and her extremities were well perfused. She had a normal mental status with appropriate speech, language, and cognition.\nThe assessment was acute gastritis, and the plan was to have her follow up in four weeks. She was told to cut back on the alcohol, alcohol, and caffeine. She was given a prescription for zantac as well as }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes type 2, asthma, and gastritis.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of moderately heavy alcohol use. She also enjoys coffee.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily, Flovent twice daily, and albuterol as-needed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. Denies diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Mild end expiratory wheezes, very faint.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium. Abdomen is soft. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Abdominal pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be acute gastritis, likely related to her increased caffeine intake. She has a history of moderately heavy alcohol use as well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed appropriate limitations for alcohol intake as well as spicy foods.\n- Medical Treatment: Reduce caffeine intake. Reduce alcohol intake. Prescription for Zantac twice daily provided. I\'m going to order a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and a comprehensive metabolic panel.\n\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n- Medical Reasoning: She is doing well, but I want to make a slight adjustment to her dosage.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue with home monitoring and bring those readings to her next appointment.\n- Medical Treatment: Metformin 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg at night.\nContinue home blood sugar monitoring.\n\n3. Asthma.\n- Medical Reasoning: She seems to be doing well on her current regimen and has not had any asthma attacks lately.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: She was advised to take Flovent daily despite no recent asthma attacks as this will decrease the frequency of her attacks.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue on daily Flovent. Continue albuterol as-needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n6. Section Relevance:\n    - Does the summary note accurately assign clinical information to the corr