ac twice daily provided. I\'m going to order a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and a comprehensive metabolic panel.\n\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n- Medical Reasoning: She is doing well, but I want to make a slight adjustment to her dosage.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue with home monitoring and bring those readings to her next appointment.\n- Medical Treatment: Metformin 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg at night.\nContinue home blood sugar monitoring.\n\n3. Asthma.\n- Medical Reasoning: She seems to be doing well on her current regimen and has not had any asthma attacks lately.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: She was advised to take Flovent daily despite no recent asthma attacks as this will decrease the frequency of her attacks.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue on daily Flovent. Continue albuterol as-needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nAbdominal pain.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nBarbara Lee is a 31-year-old female with a history of diabetes mellitus type 2 and asthma who presents with a one-week history of moderate abdominal pain. She reports that the pain is gradually increasing in severity and is associated with nausea and occasional vomiting. The nausea has been intermittent, occurring 3 times in the past week. She notes that her pain is exacerbated by physical activity and has impacted her ability to exercise, including her usual jogging routine. She has a longstanding history of coffee consumption, including frequent visits to Starbucks, and has recently increased her intake. She is highly motivated to follow medical advice and has been cooperative with previous treatments.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** No fever.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Moderate abdominal pain, nausea, occasional vomiting.\n- **Endocrine:** Blood glucose levels within controlled range, no hypo/hyperglycemic episodes reported.\n- **Respiratory:** No recent asthma attacks, uses flovent Budesonide 200 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert and oriented, no acute distress.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n- **Abdominal:** Mild tenderness in the epigastrium, no rebound tenderness, no guarding. Abdomen soft, no tenderness in the right lower quadrant, no significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n- **Respiratory:** Mild expiratory wheezes, otherwise normal lung exam, excellent air movement.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n1. **Acute Gastritis, unspecified, without bleeding.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms of abdominal pain, nausea, and occasional vomiting, along with her recent increase in coffee intake and history of alcohol use, are consistent with acute gastritis. Physical exam findings support this diagnosis.\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood glucose levels are within the controlled range, and she is currently on metformin 500 mg once daily, which was increased to 500 mg twice daily in the previous visit.\n3. **Asthma.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s asthma is currently well-controlled on flovent 200 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed. No recent exacerbations reported.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n1. **Acute Gastritis.**\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Prescribed Omeprazole 20 mg, oral, twice a day, 30 tablets.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Advised the patient to reduce caffeine and alcohol intake. Recommended cutting back on spicy food as well.\n   - **Follow-up:** Follow-up in 4 weeks.\n   - **Additional Testing:** Ordered urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and CMP to rule out other possible causes of abdominal pain.\n\n2. **Type 2 