\n- **Endocrine:** No thyromegaly, no signs of acanthosis nigricans.\n\n**Results:**\n- X-ray of the right elbow: Normal x-ray, no fracture or bony abnormality.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** Elbow contusion, right side, initial encounter.\n- **Contributing Factor:** Likely due to a lacrosse stick impact from his child.\n- **Secondary Diagnoses:**\n  - Hypertension, well-managed on current medication.\n  - Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, well-managed on current medication.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Right Elbow Contusion:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** The patient presents with moderate pain and tenderness at the tip of the right elbow, consistent with a contusion. The normal x-ray indicates no fracture.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Advised the patient to rest the elbow and avoid activities that exacerbate the pain, including playing lacrosse for the next 2 weeks.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Prescribed Ibuprofen 800 mg, oral, twice a day, as needed for pain, for 7 days. Advised to ice the elbow 3 times a day for 20 minutes to reduce swelling and pain.\n\n- **Hypertension:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** Blood pressure was elevated at 150/90 mmHg. The patient is on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Advised the patient to continue taking lisinopril as prescribed and to monitor blood pressure regularly.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** No changes to current medication at this time.\n\n- **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** The patient is on metformin 500 mg daily and reports compliance with the medication.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Advised the patient to continue taking metformin as prescribed and to monitor blood sugar levels regularly.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Ordered a biochemistry panel to assess current glucose levels. Consideration for potential dose adjustment based on results.\n\n- **Follow-up Recommendations:**\n  - Follow-up visit in 2 weeks to assess progress and response to treatment.\n  - A1C test to monitor diabetes control.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Philip is a sixty-five-year-old male, who presented to the office complaining of right elbow pain. He also has a past medical history of hypertension and diabetes mellitus. He stated that he hurt his right elbow playing lacrosse with his kids. He was playing with a thirteen -year-old and he thinks he got hit with the lacrosse stick and hurt his elbow. He was also concerned about his high blood pressure and his diabetes mellitus.\nPhysical exam showed a blood pressure of one fifty over seven over ninety. He was on twenty of lisinopril. He had a grade two out of six systolic ejection murmur. He had a clear bilaterally lung exam. He had a normal heart exam. He had a bruise and swelling at the right elbow. He had pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. He had mild pain with flexion and extension. He had normal range of motion at the elbow. He had a normal x-ray of the right elbow. X-ray showed no fracture no bony abnormality. X-ray showed a normal joint space. X-ray showed a normal distal humerus epiphysis. X-ray showed a normal radial head and the coronoid process. X-ray showed no signs of an elbow dislocation. X-ray showed no signs of an elbow fracture.\nThe patient was given a diagnosis of elbow contusion. He was told to take eight hundred milligrams of ibuprofen twice a day. He was told to ice his elbow three times a day for twenty minutes at a time. He was told not to play lacrosse with his son for the next couple of weeks. He was given a prescription for ibuprofen. He was told to follow up in a couple of weeks. He was also told to continue his hypertension and diabetes mellitus medications.\nThe patient was seen again in the office two weeks later. He stated that his elbow was feeling much better. He was told to continue taking ibuprofen and icing