e been trending high.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of heart disease.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies diaphoresis or clamminess.\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies dyspnea.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD, swelling, or carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Assessment of Respiratory Effort:\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, otherwise normal rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: No masses or tenderness. Soft, no guarding.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema in the ankles. No calf tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG was obtained and reviewed in office today and is unremarkable. Hemoglobin A1c: 7.0\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: Considering her history of diabetes, possible history of hypertension, and family history of heart disease, I suspect her chest pain is likely unstable angina. Her recent EKG was unremarkable and there are no other signs of heart attack, but I am worried she may have some sort of a blockage causing her pain.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed proper protocol for sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain. I advised her to go to the emergency department if the medication is not effective after approximately 5 minutes.\n- Medical Treatment: We will have her start a regimen of baby aspirin at 81 mg daily, for which I have sent in a prescription today. I also sent a prescription for nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed. Finally, I am going to have her follow up with cardiology for further evaluation.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: She appears to be doing well on her current regimen.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to keep her appointment with ophthalmology for evaluation of diabetic retinopathy.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin A1c to check for any needed medication adjustments and have her follow up in 2 weeks. In the meantime, I want her to continue with metformin 500 mg twice daily and dietary modifications.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Several providers, including myself, have noted a trend of elevated blood pressures in the patient\'s chart.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to monitor her blood pressures at home over the next 2 weeks and report her readings to me via MyChart. This should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to her new medications. We discussed the possible side effects of hydrochlorothiazide how this will hopefully reduce some of her swelling seen on physical exam. All of her questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to have her start hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nChest pain.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nElizabeth Johnson is a 53-year-old African American female who presents for an initial consultation with complaints of intermittent chest pain during exertion. She reports that the pain has been occurring over the past 3 months and has limited her ability to engage in physical activities. The pain is described as a dull, pressure-like sensation that is exacerbated by walking. She denies any associated symptoms such as shortness of breath, diaphoresis, or syncope. The pain is not currently present at the time of the visit.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Cardiovascular: Reports chest pain during exertion.\n- Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, cough, or wheezing.\n- Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\n- Endocrine: Known history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, currently managed with metformin.\n- Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: Elevated, noted in previous clinic visits.\n- Heart Rate: Within normal limits.\n- Respiratory Rate: Within normal limits.\n- Temperature: Within normal limits.\n- Oxygen Saturation: Within normal limits.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- General: Alert, oriented, and in no acute distress.\n- Neck: No jugular venous distention, no carotid bruits.\n- Cardiovascular: Presence of a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, regular rate and rhythm, no peripheral edema noted.\n- Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no crackles or rhonchi.\n- Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly, no masses palpated.\n- Extremities: Mild bilateral lower extremity edema, 1+ pitting edema noted.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- EKG: No signs of myocardial infarction.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n1. Chest pain, unspecified.\n   - Medical Reasoning: Given the patient\'s history of exertional chest pain and the presence of a systolic ejection murmur, the differential diagnosis includes atherosclerotic heart disease with possible angina pectoris. The chest pain could also be related to her history of hypertension and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, and her family history of ischemic heart disease.\n   \n2. Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient has a known history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, currently managed with metformin. Her hemoglobin A1c is pending.\n   \n3. Hypertension.\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient has a trend of elevated blood pressure readings in her medical history, documented by several other clinicians.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n1. Chest pain.\n   - Medical Treatment: Prescribed nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingual tablets, 1 tablet to be taken sublingually as needed for chest pain, up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes if chest pain persists. Advised to call 911 and go to the hospital if chest pain does not resolve or worsens.\n   - Referral: Referral to Cardiology for further evaluation of potential atherosclerotic heart disease. Appointment scheduled with Dr. John Smith, Cardiologist, for next week.\n   - Follow-up: Follow-up in 2 weeks to monitor response to treatment and review test results.\n\n2. Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.\n   - Medical Treatment: Continue current metformin 500 mg twice daily. Ordered hemoglobin A1c.\n   - Referral: Referral to Ophthalmology for diabetic retinopathy screening. Appointment scheduled in 2 weeks.\n   - Follow-up: Follow-up in 2 weeks to review hemoglobin A1c and ophthalmology results.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n   - Medical Treatment: Prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg once daily.\n   - Follow-up: Advised to use a home blood pressure monitor and enter readings in Epic MyChart. Will review blood pressure log at the next visit.\n\n**Patient Education and Counseling:**\n- Advised the patient on the importance of medication adherence and lifestyle modifications, including diet and exercise.\n- Discussed potential side effects of new medications and the importance of reporting any adverse reactions.\n- Emphasized the need for regular follow-up and monitoring of chronic conditions.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan and follow-up instructions. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Elizabeth is a 53 -year-old female who presented to the office complaining of chest pain. She has a past medical history of diabetes mellitus and hypertension. She stated that she has been having chest pain every time she starts walking. The pain is in the center of her chest and it feels like pressure. It is not radiating anywhere. She said she has been having this pain for three months now. She is a diabetic and she has been trying to exercise to control her diabetes but she has not been able to keep up with it because of the chest pain. She also has a family history of heart disease in her father and sister. She denied any shortness of breath, sweats, clamminess, or nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or abdominal pain. She denied any recent trauma. She denied any use of tobacco, alcohol, or illicit drugs. She did not have any recent sick contacts or travel. She denied any prior heart attacks or surgeries. She had an ekg done in the office and it did not show any signs of a heart attack. She was also on metformin 500 milligrams twice a day. She was also told to go to ophthalmology for a diabetic retinopathy exam. Her hemoglobin a1c was seven percent. She was also on lisinopril for her hypertension.\nOn exam, she was a well-appearing lady in no acute distress. Her vital signs were blood pressure 132 over 72 heart rate 78 respiratory rate 18 and oxygen saturation 98 percent on room air. Her exam was unremarkable except for mild swelling of the lower extremities with one plus nonpitting edema. She had a two over six systolic ejection murmur on her heart exam. Her lungs were clear to auscultation and percussion. Her abdomen was soft, nontender, nondistended, with no masses, guarding, or rigidity. Her extremities showed no deformities, tenderness, or swelling except for the mild swelling of the lower extremities. She did not have any peripheral neuropathy.\nThe first diagnosis that was considered was the chest pain. The second diagnosis was the diabetes. The third diagnosis was the hypertension. The plan for the chest pain was to get her into cardiology. The plan for the diabetes was to continue the metformin and follow up in two weeks. The plan for the hypertension was to start her on hydrochlorothiazide. She was told to use a blood pressure cuff at home and enter her findings at epic mychart. She was also told to continue exercising and try to control her diabetes. She was told to s