ief Complaint (CC):**\nModerate to severe low back pain with radiation down the left leg.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nKyle Thompson, a 35-year-old African American male, presents for a routine check-up with a chief complaint of moderate to severe low back pain that radiates down the left leg. The pain started two weeks ago after lifting a box of books. He reports a sensation of a "pop" during the incident. The pain is described as aching and sharp and worsens with activity. The patient has a sedentary job and is an Olympic weightlifter with books. He has tried over-the-counter pain relief with ibuprofen and tylenol with some relief. The pain has been intermittent but has significantly limited his ability to exercise and perform daily tasks. He has not discussed surgical options previously.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports moderate to severe low back pain, radiating down the left leg.\n- Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling, no loss of sensation or weakness, denies bowel or bladder incontinence.\n\n**Social History:**\n- Occupation: Sedentary office job.\n- Exercise: Inconsistent, enjoys Olympic weightlifting.\n- Tobacco Use: None.\n- Alcohol Consumption: Social drinker.\n- Recreational Activities: Soccer, Manchester United fan.\n- Family History: Father with a history of back pain.\n- Education Level: Bachelor\'s degree.\n- Socio-economic Status: Upper-middle class.\n- Neighborhood: Urban, affluent, low pollution.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 130/70 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: Afebrile\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- General: Alert, cooperative, in no acute distress.\n- Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n- Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- Musculoskeletal: No overlying redness or bruising on the skin. Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain elicited with lumbar flexion and extension.\n- Neurological: Positive straight leg test (SLS) in the supine position. No radicular signs in the left leg. Reflexes: Patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- MRI Lumbar Spine: Shows disk desiccation, bulging of the disk with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Lumbar back pain at L4-L5 disk space due to herniated disk with radiculopathy.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Medical Treatment:**\n  - Spinal injection with a combination of a strong NSAID (e.g., Ketorolac) and corticosteroid (e.g., Methylprednisolone) administered intrathecal route into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy. This should be scheduled today.\n- **Patient Education and Counseling:**\n  - Discussed the nature of the diagnosis and the importance of the spinal injection for immediate relief.\n  - Explained the procedure, potential risks, and benefits.\n  - Emphasized the importance of adhering to the prescribed treatment and follow-up appointments.\n- **Follow-up:**\n  - Follow-up appointment in one week to assess the efficacy of the injection and to discuss further management.\n- **Referral:**\n  - Referral to Dr. Emily Johnson, a neurologist, for further evaluation of the radiculopathy.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ A 25-year-old male, Olympic weightlifter with books, presented to clinic with acute onset of low back pain after lifting. The patient reported that he felt a “pop” while lifting and was unsure what was occurring. He stated that he did not use proper lifting technique. The patient described the pain as achy and sharp in his low back. He stated that the pain did not radiate at first but now is starting to shoot down his left leg. The patient stated that positioning did not bother him, and he did not have numbness, tingling, or los