\nRegarding her epilepsy, she reports that she has not had any recent seizures. She reports that her last seizure was a year ago. She reports that she is still taking Keppra.\n\nRegarding her depression, she reports that she started therapy last year and she is trying to avoid medications for this problem. She reports that therapy has been helpful, but she still feels down and stressed. She denies suicidal and homicidal ideations.\n\nRegarding her peptic ulcer, she reports that she was dealing with a lot of stress last year. She then underwent an endoscopy which showed that she had an episode of gastritis. She was subsequently diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease and started Protonix. She reports that she has not had any issues with her ulcer since the procedure and everything has been good.\n\nThe patient denies chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting. She reports that she does have a little bit of medial nasal congestion from allergies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Ears, Nose, Mouth and Throat: Endorses medial nasal congestion from allergies.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow joint pain, swelling, and weakness.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow reveals erythema and edema. Pain to palpation of the left olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLeft arm x-ray does not reveal any evidence of elbow fracture.\n\nEGD revealed an episode of gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAnna Morris is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with some joint pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: I believe she has left olecranon bursitis caused by repetitive trauma.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate meloxicam 15 mg once daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAnna M. Rodriguez is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents to the clinic with left elbow pain. The patient reports that her elbow has been stiff and painful, particularly after work, and she has difficulty opening and closing her left elbow. The pain is worse with use. She denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in her hands. She reports a little bit of nasal congestion from allergies. She denies fever or chills. The patient reports that she has been doing well with her epilepsy, with her last seizure occurring one year ago. She is currently taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day. She also reports that she has been doing well with her depression, and she has been in therapy. She has been able to avoid medication since she is already on Keppra. She has been doing better with her depression, but she still feels stressed and down. She reports that she has not felt like hurting herself or anyone else. The patient denies any chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea or vomiting.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain. Denies numbness, tingling, or weakness in the hands.\n• Neurological: Denies seizures. Denies depression.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea or vomiting. Endoscopy showed gastritis.\n• ENT: Endorses nasal congestion.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• General: Well-appearing female in no acute distress.\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow: Erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain to palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• X-ray of the left elbow: No evidence of fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Left elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with left elbow pain, which is consistent with olecranon bursitis. She has a history of repetitive trauma to the area. I recommend a little pillow for her elbow to provide support. She will take meloxicam 15 mg once daily. She can ice the area. She will follow up in 2 weeks.\n• Epilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her epilepsy, with her last seizure occurring one year ago. She is currently taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day. She will continue this medication.\n• Depression.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her depression, but she still feels stressed and down. She has been in therapy. She has been able to avoid medication since she is already on Keppra. I would like to start her on Lexapro 10 mg once a day. We will see how she does with this medication.\n• Peptic Ulcer Disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of peptic ulcer disease. She had an episode of gastritis on her endoscopy. She will continue on Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with left elbow pain.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Keppra 500 mg, twice a day\n• Protonix 40 mg, once a day before meals\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain. Endorses stiffness, swelling, and erythema on the left elbow. Denies weakness or tingling in the left arm.\n• Neurological: Denies seizures.\n• Psychiatric: Denies depression, but endorses depression and anxiety. Endorses feeling down and stressed.\n• Respiratory: Endorses nasal congestion.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• General: In the office today in a cooperative and pleasant manner. In no acute distress.\n• Musculoskeletal: Left elbow: Erythema and edema. Pain to palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Left elbow x-ray: No evidence of any elbow fracture.\n• Endoscopy: Episode of gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Left elbow pain.\n2. Epilepsy.\n3. Depression.\n4. Peptic ulcer disease.\n\nPLAN\n\n1. Left elbow pain: We discussed the patient\'s left elbow pain. She is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with left elbow pain. She endorses stiffness, swelling, and erythema on the left elbow. She denies weakness or tingling in the left arm. She has no fever or chills. She is right-handed. She has pain with flexion and extension. We reviewed the left elbow x-ray and there is no evidence of any elbow fracture. I have advised her to use ice and a pillow for her elbow. I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg, once a day.\n2. Epilepsy: We discussed the patient\'s epilepsy. She is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents today with left elbow pain. She reports that she has not had a seizure in the last year. She is currently taking kepp