k. She reports that she has struggled with high blood pressure in the past, but it has been getting out of control lately. She states that she is currently taking Norvasc 10 mg daily. She also reports occasional headaches, which she is unsure if are related to the high blood pressure or increased stress at work. She denies any chest pain, shortness of breath, or other concerning symptoms. She reports that she has a history of kidney transplant 3 years ago, and she is currently compliant with her immunosuppressive medications.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n• Neurological: Reports occasional headaches, unsure if related to blood pressure or increased stress at work.\n• Renal: Denies any symptoms.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Blood Pressure: 162/95 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• General: Appears well-nourished, no acute distress.\n• Neck: Supple.\n• Cardiovascular: Irregular rate and rhythm. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Musculoskeletal: Positive scar on the left flank from previous kidney transplant.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Creatinine: 1.2 mg/dL.\n• EKG: Normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMs. Brown is a 26-year-old female who presents for a high blood pressure check. Her blood pressure is not well controlled on Norvasc 10 mg daily. She reports occasional headaches. Her kidney function is stable 3 years post-transplant.\n\nMedical Reasoning\n\nThe patient\'s blood pressure is not well controlled on current medications. We will continue her current Norvasc 10 mg daily and add Carvedilol 25 mg, twice daily. I also want to order a lipid panel and a complete metabolic panel to ensure that everything is okay.\n\nPatient Education\n\nI explained the risks and benefits of the new medication and the importance of taking it as prescribed. I also advised her to continue her current immunosuppressive medications.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks to check her blood pressure. }