n.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she has been stressed at work and has been putting in more hours, which has led to a poor diet and limited time for grocery shopping and meal preparation.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports she has been taking her medications as prescribed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports carpal tunnel release done 2 years ago, no numbness or tingling in hands.\nNeurological: Reports headaches, which she attributes to prolonged computer use.\nPsychiatric: Denies any suicidal or homicidal ideation.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\nNeurological: Alert and oriented, no focal deficits, equal and normal reflexes.\nMusculoskeletal: No focal tenderness or swelling.\nMusculoskeletal: No focal tenderness or swelling.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLabs: All normal.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Hypertension.\n2. Depression.\n3. Carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nPatient will increase her Lisinopril to 10 mg once daily. We will check her blood pressure in 1 month and continue to monitor for any signs of worsening hypertension. She will continue to take her current medications for depression and continue to attend therapy weekly. She will continue to follow up as needed for carpal tunnel syndrome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHx of hypertension.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she has been stressed with work and putting in more hours, so she has been eating a lot of frozen meals.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient is currently taking lisinopril.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she had carpal tunnel release done 2 years ago.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or night sweats.\nEyes: Denies visual changes, eye pain.\nENT: Denies ear pain, hearing loss.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath, dyspnea on exertion.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain, dyspnea on exertion.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain, muscle weakness.\nSkin: Denies rashes, itching.\nNeurological: Denies headache, tingling, numbness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 134/86 mmHg, 146/86 mmHg.\nPulse: 72.\nRespiratory rate: 16.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In general appearance well-nourished.\nNeuro: Neurologically intact. No focal neurologic deficits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nBlood pressure in office today was elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Hypertension.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient reports she has been stressed with work and putting in more hours, so she has been eating a lot of frozen meals. She denies headache, tingling, or numbness. Her blood pressure is elevated. She is currently taking lisinopril. I am increasing her lisinopril to 10 mg once daily. We will recheck her blood pressure in a month.\n2. Depression.\nMedical Reasoning: Patient reports she is doing well with her depression. She is currently taking the medication and seeing a therapist. We will continue to monitor her symptoms.\n3. Carpal tunnel syndrome.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient reports she is doing well. She is asymptomatic. We will continue to monitor for any symptoms.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nPatient will increase her lisinopril to 10 mg once daily. We will recheck her blood pressure in a month. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAnnual exam.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristine Flores is a 63-year-old female presenting for her yearly exam. She has a history of high blood pressure and depression in the past. She also had a right carpal tunnel release done about 2 years ago.\n\nThe patient states that she has been doing well with her depression. She notes that she has been taking her medication as prescribed at her last visit. She states that she has been going to therapy every week, which has helped her manage her symptoms. The patient notes that she is sleeping well at night and has been getting a full 8 hours. She denies any thoughts of self-harm or harming others.\n\nThe patient states that she has been checking h