hma, uncomplicated\nStart Alvesco, 2 puffs twice daily. Keep Wixela in case Alvesco does not work well. Continue albuterol as needed. Return in 4 months to repeat ACT and spirometry testing.\n\nAllergic rhinitis\nContinue Zyrtec 10 mg daily as needed.\n\nAnaphylactic reaction due to peanuts\nContinue strict avoidance of peanuts. I will refill her EpiPen today.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAsthma, allergic rhinitis, food allergy.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she was switched from Symbicort to Wixela due to insurance changes. She uses Zyrtec in the spring, summer, and fall for seasonal allergy symptoms. She does well with it. She has a known peanut allergy, but tolerates almonds and cashews.\n\nALLERGIES\n\nPatient reports she is allergic to peanuts. She is allergic to tree nuts. She is not allergic to almonds and cashews.\n\nASTHMA\n\nPatient reports she is currently on Wixela 250/50 twice a day. She has not used her albuterol inhaler much lately. She reports she has had to use it occasionally before exercise. She has been trying to use it less often as she is trying to join the military. She reports she has not had any urgent care or ER visits for breathing in the past year. She has not had any steroid use for breathing issues.\n\nPATIENT EDUCATION AND AGREEMENT\n\nPatient was advised that she will need to have her asthma regimen reviewed as she is currently on Wixela 250/50 twice a day. She was advised that she may be able to use it once a day if she is doing well. She was advised that she may be able to switch to an inhaler that has one medication in it, Alvesco. She was advised that she may need to use it twice a day. She was advised that she needs to continue to use her albuterol as needed. She was advised that she needs to continue to use her epipen. She was advised to repeat her spirometry in 4 months. She understands and agrees with the plan.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n- Alvesco 80 mcg twice a day.\n- Albuterol inhaler as needed.\n- Epipen.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n- The patient was advised to use the Alvesco 80 mcg twice a day.\n- She was advised to use her albuterol as needed.\n- She was advised to continue to use her epipen.\n- She was advised to repeat her spirometry in 4 months.\n- She was advised to follow up in 4 months with spirometry and ACT.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAsthma, moderate severity, controlled with current regimen.\n\nPLAN\n\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 20-year-old female with a history of asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergy. She was switched from Symbicort to Wixela due to insurance changes. She uses Zyrtec in the spring, summer, and fall for seasonal allergy symptoms. She does well with it. She has a known peanut allergy, but tolerates almonds and cashews. She reports she is currently on Wixela 250/50 twice a day. She has not used her albuterol inhaler much lately. She reports she has had to use it occasionally before exercise. She has been trying to use it less often as she is trying to join the military. She reports she has not had any urgent care or ER visits for breathing in the past year. She has not had any steroid use for breathing issues.\n- Medical Treatment: The patient was advised to continue on Wixela 250/50 twice a day. She was advised that she may be able to use it once a day if she is doing well. She was advised that she may be able to switch to an inhaler that has one medication in it, Alvesco. She was advised that she may need to use it twice a day. She was advised that she needs to continue to use her albuterol as needed. She was advised that she needs to continue to use her epipen. She was advised to repeat her spirometry in 4 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAsthma.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nEmily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female who presents for a follow-up of her asthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been doing well since her last office visit in 2020.