a and Vomiting.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBrittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome. She was prescribed Bentyl, which she uses as needed for intestinal cramping. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She states that she has been having some cramping and nausea. She denies any vomiting.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been having a bowel movement 2-3 times a day. She states that they are formed. She notes that after she goes out, she tends to have diarrhea. She states that she is not sure if it is the food or the alcohol or both.\n\nShe reports that she usually eats pasta, the chicken alfredo at Friday\'s restaurant. She notes that it normally does not bother her. She states that she usually drinks tequila or dark liquor. She notes that she prefers Patron. She states that she only gets two drinks every now and then. She notes that if she gets brown liquor, she only gets two. She notes that she will drink a martini sometimes. She states that it is the mixer they put in the drinks that tends to bother her.\n\nThe patient reports that when she is not eating out or drinking, what she eats on a daily basis does help her stay regular. When it does flare up, it is not as bad. She notes that she is still having cramping and sharp pains, so she has been avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she did have some fried chicken today.\n\nThe patient reports that she has not been taking the Citrucel regularly.\n\nThe patient reports that she is having some cramping and nausea. She notes that she is still having diarrhea. She notes that she is avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she had some fried chicken today. She states that she feels like she can not go out without some fried chicken at least once in a while.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nConstitutional: In no apparent distress.\n\nCV: Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nGastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender abdomen. No masses or organomegaly. Normal active bowel sounds.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNone.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBrittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome. She is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome. She was prescribed Bentyl, which she uses as needed for intestinal cramping. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She states that she has been having some cramping and nausea. She denies any vomiting.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been having a bowel movement 2-3 times a day. She states that they are formed. She notes that after she goes out, she tends to have diarrhea. She states that she is not sure if it is the food or the alcohol or both.\n\nThe patient reports that she usually eats pasta, the chicken alfredo at Friday\'s restaurant. She notes that it normally does not bother her. She states that she usually drinks tequila or dark liquor. She notes that she prefers Patron. She states that she only gets two drinks every now and then. She notes that if she gets brown liquor, she only gets two. She notes that she will drink a martini sometimes. She states that it is the mixer they put in the drinks that tends to bother her.\n\nThe patient reports that when she is not eating out or drinking, what she eats on a daily basis does help her stay regular. When it does flare up, it is not as bad. She notes that she is still having cramping and sharp pains, so she has been avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she did have some fried chicken today.\n\nThe patient reports that she has not been taking the Citrucel regularly.\n\nThe patient reports that she is having some cramping and nausea. She notes that she is still having diarrhea. She notes that she is avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she had some fried chicken today. She states that she feels like she can not go out without some fried chicken at least once in a while.\n\nI have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications. I have started her on Elavil 25 milligrams at bedtime for irritable bowel syndrome. She can use Bentyl 20 milligrams as needed 30-45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed. I have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption. I have asked her to follow up in one month by emailing over MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who returns in follow up for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nMs. Edwards was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain and was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome that was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as need