 and fatty foods and alcohol consumption . i have asked her to follow up in one month by emailing over mychart regarding any side effects and issues , or issues with elavil or if adjustments are needed .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who returns in follow up for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nMs. Edwards was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain and was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome that was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement such as Citrucel daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, and Bentyl up to 4 times daily for intestinal cramping. She was instructed to discontinue Reglan and to use Zofran as needed for nausea. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nMs. Edwards continues to have intermittent abdominal symptoms. Monday through Thursday she has less flare ups of her symptoms but states she is currently having cramps and sharp pains. She describes she recently ate shrimp which caused her to have both upper and lower abdominal pain and bloating. However, she notices episodes of diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo and consuming alcohol. She does not believe the alfredo contributes to her symptoms. The patient states she consumes 2 drinks when out of either tequila or brown liquor and occasionally a martini. She does her best to avoid “heavy foods,” but ate fried chicken today without any symptoms. She has stopped drinking sodas which has been helpful.\u202fThe patient reports passing 2-3 formed stools a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYMPTOMS\n\n•\u202fGastrointestinal:\u202fPositive nausea, diarrhea, and abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nAlert and cooperative, appears stated age.\n\nRespiratory\nLungs clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nHeart regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\nAbdomen soft, non-tender, no masses or organomegaly. Normoactive bowel sounds.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Irritable bowel syndrome\n\nBrittany Edwards is a 32-year-old established patient who returned today to discuss management of IBS and functional abdominal pain made worse by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications as well as starting a neuromodulator, such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use\u202fBentyl\u202f20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes\u202fbefore eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran as needed for nausea. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy fatty foods and alcohol consumption.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSend MyChart message in 1 month regarding any side effects of issues with Elavil or if dose adjustments are needed.\nReturn to clinic in 1 month or sooner if needed.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nIBS with functional abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient is a pleasant, 32-year-old female who was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) and treated with Bentyl.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient was previously prescribed Reglan which she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to 4 times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. Denies hematochezia or melena.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness.\nNeurological: Denies headaches or dizziness.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: Alert and cooperative, appears stated age.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.\nGastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender, no masses or organomegaly. Active bowel sounds present.\n\nRESULTS\n\nN/A\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBrittany Edwards is a pleasant female with Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) and functional abdominal pain, aggravated by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits. I have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications, as well as starting a neuromodulator such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI have instructed the patient to follow up in one month by email through MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: I provided the patient with information about her condition and the recommended medical treatment plan. I also encouraged her to watch out for greasy or fatty foods and sugary drinks, as these can trigger symptoms.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nBentyl 20 mg, 60 tablets, refilled.\nElavil 25 mg, 30 tablets, new prescription.\nZofran 4 mg, 10 tablets, refilled.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in one month by email through MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nIrritable Bowel Syndrome with Functional Abdominal Pain, Nausea and Vomiting.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBrittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome. She was prescribed Bentyl, which she uses as needed for intestinal cramping. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She states that she has been having some cramping and nausea. She denies any vomiting.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been having a bowel movement 2-3 times a day. She states that they are formed. She notes that after she goes out, she tends to have diarrhea. She states that she is not sure if it is the food or the alcohol or both.\n\nShe reports that she usually eats pasta, the chicken alfredo at Friday\'s restaurant. She notes that it normally does not bother her. She states that she usually drinks tequila or dark liquor. She notes that she prefers Patron. She states that she only gets two drinks every now and then. She notes that if she gets brown liquor, she only gets two. She notes that she will drink a martini sometimes. She states that it is the mixer they put in the drinks that tends to bother her.\n\nThe patient reports that when she is not eating out or drinking, what she eats on a daily basis does help her stay regular. When it does flare up, it is not as bad. She notes that she is still having cramping and sharp pains, so she has been avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she did have some fried chicken today.\n\nThe patient reports that she has not been taking the Citrucel regularly.\n\nThe patient reports 