Blood pressure 124/80, heart rate is 64, respiratory rate is 18, weight 174 pounds, and pain is 0/10.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nMr. Watson is a pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily though his daughter adds some details.\n\nHead and Face\nThe shunt site was clean, dry, and intact and confirmed at a setting of 2.0.\n\nNeurological\nMental status: Tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, concentration, and fund of knowledge. He scored 26/30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25/30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get two of the three memory words with cuing and the third one with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling and at that time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing and only one with multiple choice.\nGait: Tested using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance of score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This has slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28.\nCranial Nerves: Pupils are equal. Extraocular movements are intact. Face symmetric. No dysarthria.\nMotor: Normal for bulk and strength.\nCoordination: Slow for finger-to-nose.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT scan of the head from 10/15/2020: It shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that is flat with the span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. This was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Adult hydrocephalus.\n• Gait impairment.\n• Urinary incontinence and urgency.\n• Cognitive impairment.\n\nMr. Watson has made some improvement with his hydrocephalus since shunt surgery. There is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. I do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic, and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the CT scan.\n\nMr. Watson asked me about whether he will be able to drive again. Unfortunately, I think it is unlikely that his speed of movement will improve to a level that he will be able to pass a driver\'s safety evaluation, however, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over 9 to 12 months that they are able to pass such a test. I would certainly be happy to recommend such a test if I believe he is likely to pass it and he is always welcome to enroll in a driver\'s safety program without my recommendation, however, I think it is exceeding unlikely that he has the capability of passing this rigorous test at this time. I also think it is quite likely he will not regain sufficient speed of motion to pass such a test.\n\nPLAN\n\nI had a long discussion with the patient and his daughter. We are all pleased that he has started to make some improvement with his hydrocephalus because of the fluid collection in the right parietal region, I would like to leave the setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing the shunt.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow up in 3 months.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns for follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. He initially presented in May 2020 with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. He underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020, and was placed on a Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration, which was set at 2.0.\n\nThe patient reports that his walking has improved and he is able to walk to the dining room without assistance. However, he still has times where he needs help. His bladder control has improved but has leveled off, and he is now wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores from wearing the diaper, or decreased or painful urination. He notes that his cognitive function has improved, stating he no longer feels lost when talking to someone. He denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is, and any abdominal pain.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nShunt.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.\nGenitourinary: Urinary incontinence and urgency. Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output.\nNeurological: Continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.\nPsychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 124/80.\nHeart rate: 64.\nRespirations: 18.\nWeight: 174 pounds.\nPain: 0/10.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In no acute distress.\nNeurological: Alert and oriented. Cranial nerves II-XII intact. No focal deficits.\nMusculoskeletal: Ambulatory without assistive device. Gait score 6-8 out of 12, balance score 12 out of 16 for a total of 18 to 20 out of 28. Slightly improved from initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT scan of the head from 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Adult hydrocephalus.\n2. Gait impairment.\n3. Urinary incontinence and urgency.\n4. Cognitive impairment.\n\nPLAN\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. We will continue to monitor his improvement and will likely leave the shunt set at 2.0 for the next 2-3 months. The patient was advised that he may be able to drive again if his movement speed improves. He was also advised that he could enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents for follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. He was first seen in May 2020 with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus and he underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been doing much better since his surgery. His walking has improved. He is now able to walk to the dining room. However, he still has times when he needs assistance. His bladder control has improved, but has leveled out. He is now wearing diapers. He denies any sores from wearing the diaper, or any decreased or painful urination. He also denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical History: Adult hydrocephalus.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nShunt surgery on August 1, 2020.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient lives with his daughter, Theresa. She is the one who helps him keep things straight.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that his sister, Hannah, summarized his condition well. She stated that he can have a normal conversation with him again.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss. Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting. Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Neurological: Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations. Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: No clubbing, cyanosis, or clubbing. No jaundice. No lymphadenopathy. No rash.\nGastrointestinal: Bowel sounds present. No masses, tenderness, or guarding.\nGenitourinary: No masses, tenderness, or guarding. No blood in the urine.\nNeurological: Cranial nerves II-XII intact. Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination slow from finger to nose.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT of the head on 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAdult hydrocephalus. Gait impairment. Urinary incontinence and urgency. Cognitive impairment.\n\nPatient was reviewed and counselled regarding his recent CT scan. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. However, he is not experiencing any symptoms at this time and I do not believe it is causing any symptoms. I would like to leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before we go in and change it.\n\nI do not believe his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass the driver\'s test. However, he could definitely improve over the next 9-12 months and at that point we can continue to discuss this. He could also enroll in a driver\'s safety program without my recommendation. He will need to continue to use a cane for ambulation.\n\nPatient will continue to follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up adult hydrocephalus, 3 months status post shunt surgery.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Christopher Watson i