. I explained that I will give him another steroid injection and order an x-ray of his right knee. He will follow up with his primary care provider and we will set up physical therapy. If he does not respond to these interventions, he will need a surgery consult.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care provider. He will continue Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. He will try to reduce his salt intake and elevate his legs. He will also consider physical therapy.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and steroid injection procedure of the right knee. The risks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia, among others. Verbal consent was attained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach. 3 mL of initially clear fluid, then blood-tinged fluid. The right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg of triamcinolone and 1 mL of lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post-procedure instructions were given. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTyler Nelson is a pleasant 72-year-old male with a past medical history significant for rheumatoid arthritis, presents today for ongoing management of his right knee pain. He notes that his knee pain has been ongoing for 2 months, with the onset following his previous right knee steroid injection. The pain is currently 3/10. He notes that the pain is worse on the right side and that he has been using a cane for support. The right knee is swollen and painful to palpation. The patient notes that he has been taking his Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily as prescribed. He also notes that he has been wearing compression socks, but they have not been helpful.\n\nHe denies any other symptoms.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nXeljanz 11 mg daily\nCelebrex 200 mg daily\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the right lower extremity reveals pitting edema of the right ankle and knee. Normal capillary refill in the right foot. The right knee is warm to the touch and has decreased range of motion. There is pain with palpation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were ordered.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right knee pain.\n2. Right knee swelling.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have discussed the patient\'s options with him and have recommended another steroid injection to the right knee. He will also undergo an x-ray of the right knee to evaluate for the cause of his edema. I have ordered physical therapy for the patient. If the patient\'s symptoms do not improve, we will discuss the need for a surgical consult.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nPatient will follow up with his PCP. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen by me on 03/04/2021, where he received a steroid injection to his right knee. He continues to experience right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6/10. He notes when he stands after he has been sitting for a while, he has to use a cane to "get his bearings." The injection he received to the right knee at the last visit was only beneficial for a few weeks. The patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia among others. Verbal consent was obtained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid then blood-tinged fluid. Then the right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post procedure instructions were given.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis management.\n• High risk medication use.\n• Right knee pain.\n• Lower extremity pitting edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\nThe patient will continue Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily. He will also continue Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nHigh-risk medication use.\nThe patient is currently on the high-risk medication Xeljanz. We will check his CMP levels and monitor periodically every couple of months.\n\nRight knee pain.\nHe has right knee pain and swelling, with pain level 6/10. On exam today, the right knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. We performed a fluid aspiration of the right knee today, see procedure note above. A steroid injection to the right knee was given today, see procedure note above. We will arrange for x-ray of the right knee and refer his to physical therapy.\n\nLower extremity pitting edema.\nThe patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not seen his PCP. We discussed compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. He will check with his PCP for further instructions.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Prof