itis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcne.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 31-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her acne. She reports that she has been doing well on her previous medications, but she has plateaued and has had a small amount of residual rash. She was taking doxycycline 100 mg once a day and was experiencing a plateau. She states that she has been using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day and the metro cream after washing. She also states that she has been using it once a day and it has been helping. She is not breastfeeding.\n\nThe patient is unsure of the duration of her acne. She states that she has had acne on and off for as long as she can remember. She has tried a variety of treatments in the past, including antibiotics and retinoids, which have not been effective. She has been using the sulfacetamide wash and the metro cream for the past 2 weeks.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Doxycycline 100 mg once a day.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\n• Patient is not currently breastfeeding.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Temperature: Not recorded.\n• Blood pressure: Not recorded.\n• Heart rate: Not recorded.\n• Respiratory rate: Not recorded.\n• Saturation: Not recorded.\n• Height: Not recorded.\n• Weight: Not recorded.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Fitzpatrick skin type IV. \n• Erythematous papules on the bilateral medial cheeks and redness around and underneath the nostrils. No other lesions.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis.\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea.\n\nPLAN\n\nI advised the patient to continue using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day and to apply the metro cream once a day. I recommended increasing the doxycycline to 100 mg twice a day. I also advised her to continue the use of the face wash and the metro cream. I explained that she can use the tacrolimus ointment if she wants to try it. I also explained that she can continue using the doxycycline for another week and then stop. I provided her with a few samples of vanicream for moisturizer. I advised her to stay away from any anti-aging or plant-based products. I will see her again in 4 weeks for follow-up.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n• Continue to use the sulfacetamide wash twice a day.\n• Continue to use the metro cream once a day.\n• Increase the doxycycline to 100 mg twice a day.\n• Use the tacrolimus ointment once a day until the rash is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping.\n• Continue to use the face wash and the metro cream after the rash is gone.\n• Use vanicream for moisturizer.\n• Stay away from anti-aging or plant-based products.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRosacea.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a pleasant 45-year-old female who presents today for a follow-up of her perioral dermatitis. She was previously seen in the clinic in 2020 for the same diagnosis. She reports that her rash is currently under good control. She has been using the doxycycline twice a day. She started to reduce the frequency of use to once a day and noticed that her rash plateaued. She has been using the doxycycline once a day for the past week. She has noticed a small amount of leftover rash. She denies any breast-feeding.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, weight loss.\nSkin: Denies pruritus, pain.\nRespiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath.\nEyes: Denies eye pain, discharge.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the skin shows Fitzpatrick skin type IV. On the bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few faint erythematous papules and a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis.\n• Rosacea.\n\nPLAN\n\n• Continue doxycycline 100 mg twice a day until the rash is gone, and then continue for another week.\n• Apply tacrolimus ointment to the rash on the face twice daily until the rash is gone.\n• Continue using the face wash.\n• Once the rash is gone, use the tacrolimus ointment once a day for another week and then stop.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI want you to gently wash your eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as Vanicream. Also, use the Bruder mask as needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n• Continue doxycycline 100 mg twice a day until the rash is gone, and then continue for another week.\n• Apply tacrolimus ointment to the rash on the face twice daily until the rash is gone.\n• Continue using the face wash.\n• Once the rash is gone, use the tacrolimus ointment once a day for another week and then stop.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI want you to gently wash your eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as Vanicream. Also, use the Bruder mask as needed. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for perioral dermatitis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. She also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. Today, she states that her perioral dermatitis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week,