: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadrant pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Jackson is a pleasant 64-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The pain has been present for several weeks and was initially diagnosed as gallstones by Dr. Miller. He has been advised to avoid high-fat foods, but he has not had any improvement in his symptoms. He denies fevers, chills, or vomiting. He has nausea and right upper quadrant pain that is severe and occurs after meals. He rates his pain as an 8/10. He has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been diet controlled. He has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. He enjoys photography and hiking. He has a family history of gallbladder disease.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nGallbladder surgery.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nHe enjoys photography and hiking.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nBoth parents had gallbladder surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills.\nGastrointestinal: Reports nausea, right upper quadrant pain.\nEndocrine: Denies polyuria, polydipsia.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nRespiratory: Respiratory rate 16, no wheezing, rales, or rhonchi.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\nAbdomen: Soft, no distension. Positive bowel sounds. Positive Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nUltrasound of the abdomen: Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBobby Jackson is a 64-year-old male who presents with a history of right upper quadrant pain. He was initially diagnosed with gallstones by Dr. Miller. He has been advised to avoid high-fat foods, but he has not had any improvement in his symptoms. He has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been diet controlled. He has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. He enjoys photography and hiking. He has a family history of gallbladder disease.\n\nGiven the lack of improvement in his symptoms with dietary modifications, I believe the patient has mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but his gallbladder is inflamed. Given this, I am recommending a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. This procedure will involve making a few small incisions and inserting a scope with instruments to remove the gallbladder. The procedure will take about an hour to an hour and a half. He will likely spend the night in the hospital for postoperative monitoring. He should avoid high-fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber.\n\nThe patient has no questions about the assessment and plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nHe will follow up in one week for postoperative care. He will see his primary care provider for diabetes follow-up. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadrant abdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Jackson is a pleasant 62-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain. The patient reports that this has been going on for several weeks. He denies fevers, chills, and weight loss. He has had nausea and vomiting. He has been avoiding fatty foods. He has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months. He reports that his symptoms have gotten better with his diet. He also notes that he is still having pain. He has a family history of gallbladder disease.\n\nThe patient endorses the pain on the right side of his belly, underneath his ribs. It feels like a squeezing severe pain. It comes and goes. When it comes it is severe and he gets nauseated and sick to his stomach. He rates the pain as an 8 out of 10. The pain is usually worse after meals, but it can also come on a couple of hours after meals.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been able to continue with his exercise and photography.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, and weight loss.\n• Gastrointestinal: Endorses nausea and vomiting. Denies diarrhea.\n• Endocrine: Reports type 2 diabetes.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, clicks, or murmurs.\n• Abdomen: Soft, nontender abdomen. Positive bowel sounds. Slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. Positive Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Abdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Cholecystitis with gallstones.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right upper quadrant abdominal pain. He denies fevers, chills, and weight loss. He has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months. He reports that his symptoms have gotten better with his diet. He has a family history of gallbladder disease. His abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder and mild thickening of the gallbladder. Given the persistent symptoms and the presence of gallstones, I recommend surgical intervention. We will proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. This is a minimally invasive procedure that will involve making a few small incisions and inserting a scope with instruments to remove the gallbladder through one of those small incisions. The procedure will take about an hour to an hour and a half. He will spend the night in the hospital for postoperative monitoring. He will need to avoid high-fat foods after surgery. We will continue to monitor his type 2 diabetes. He will follow up with his endocrinologist or primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his endocrinologist or primary care provider. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs no