 1 year and has not responded to antibiotics. She denies pain but reports decreased sensation in her foot. She has been prescribed Colganase ointment and will continue to wear a surgical shoe. We will re-evaluate in 2 weeks for further debridement.\n• Diabetes.\n• The patient has type 2 diabetes and her last A1C was 10. She denies taking her medications regularly and notes that she has been eating out frequently. She is advised to continue with her current medications and to follow up with her primary care physician to get her blood sugars under better control.\n• Abnormal Ankle-Brachial Index.\n• We will order an ABI to evaluate the blood supply to her right foot.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks for further debridement. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nNonhealing foot ulcer.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she is a smoker and a social drinker.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she has had no surgical procedures.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs are stable.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the lower extremities: 1+ edema to the right foot, no erythema, no cellulitis. There is a 1 cm x 2 cm circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, proximal to the 5th MTP joint. The wound has yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. There is no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection. There is no necrosis, no bony exposure. The wound does not hurt when touched. There is no odor.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right foot: No evidence of osteomyelitis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Nonhealing diabetic foot ulcer.\n\nPLAN\n\nPatient will undergo an ABI today. She will have a debridement today. She will be placed on Colaganase ointment to be applied to the wound once daily, covered with a sterile dressing. The patient will be placed in a surgical shoe. The patient will follow up in 2 weeks.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and ag