ered her gastritis. She denies nausea and vomiting, and states that she has experienced nausea a few times in the past week. She denies vomiting. She notes that she has vomited a couple of times. She states that her pain is on both sides of her upper abdomen, and that it worsens with exercise. She denies fever.\n• Medical Treatment: I discussed the patient\'s symptoms and medical history with her. I recommended that she cut back on her coffee intake and spicy food. I also recommended that she take Zantac 150 mg twice a day. I ordered a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, and a comprehensive metabolic panel. I also recommended that she follow up with me in 4 weeks.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a history of diabetes type 2.\n• Medical Treatment: I recommended that she continue her metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n3. Asthma.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a history of asthma.\n• Medical Treatment: I recommended that she continue her Flovent 50 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes type 2, asthma, and gastritis.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of moderately heavy alcohol use. She also enjoys coffee.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily, Flovent twice daily, and albuterol as-needed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. Denies diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Mild end expiratory wheezes, very faint.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium. Abdomen is soft. No tenderness in the righ