 day for 20 minutes at a time. I recommended that he take ibuprofen 800 mg twice a day as needed for pain. I advised him that he should avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks.\n- Medical Treatment: I prescribed ibuprofen 800 mg twice a day.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with a blood pressure of 150/90. He is currently on lisinopril 20 mg daily. He states that he has been compliant with his medications. He states that he has been eating a Mediterranean diet.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I reviewed the patient\'s options for his blood pressure. I advised him that he is doing well with his current regimen.\n- Medical Treatment: No changes were made to his current medical treatment.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with diabetes. His most recent blood sugar was 80-100. He is currently on metformin 500 mg daily. He states that he has been compliant with his medications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I reviewed the patient\'s options for his diabetes. I advised him that he is doing well with his current regimen.\n- Medical Treatment: No changes were made to his current medical treatment.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI advised the patient to follow up in two weeks to check his blood pressure and to check his blood sugar. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension but otherwise normal range of motion. There is swelling about the elbow, as well as mild ecchymosis.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examinati