NS\n\n• Bumex 2 mg oral, daily\n• Norvasc 5 mg oral, daily\n• Cozaar 100 mg oral, daily\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports that he has been sleeping well and having a good afternoon nap.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Blood Pressure: 128/72\n• Heart Rate: 79\n• Respiratory Rate: 26\n• Oxygen Saturation: 99%\n• Neck: No jugular venous distension\n• Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable\n• Lungs: Clear bilaterally\n• Extremities: Trace edema in the lower extremities\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Echocardiogram: Preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Congestive heart failure, acute on chronic.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion in uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised to continue watching his diet and to avoid salty foods. He was also advised to continue taking his medications as prescribed.\n• Medical Treatment: The patient will continue on bumex 2 mg daily and furosemide 40 mg daily.\n• Follow-Up: The patient will follow up in 3 months.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: The patient will continue on cozaar 100 mg oral, daily and norvasc 5 mg oral, daily.\n\n3. Kidney disease, chronic.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Treatment: The patient will continue to follow up with me in 3 months. I will order a renal artery ultrasound to rule out any secondary causes.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute on chronic CHF.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Kevin is an 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease who presents for follow-u