ks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess his progress and to determine his return to play date. He is advised to call the office should his symptoms change or worsen prior to his next appointment.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Jones is a 48-year-old male who presents to the emergency department with right ankle pain after a fall on the ice. The patient reports that he was walking out to take the trash and slipped on the ice. He states that he was being very careful but must have stepped on a patch of ice and slipped. He denies numbness in his foot. He has been able to put a little weight on the foot but is still limping. He has been resting it and icing it. He has taken ibuprofen for the pain. He reports that it is a little better today.\n\nThe patient is a soccer player and has been playing in the community league. He reports that his kids come and watch him play. He notes that his oldest child is now able to play soccer as well.\n\nThe patient states that he is doing well otherwise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness in the right foot.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/78 mmHg\nHeart rate: 78 bpm\nRespiratory rate: 16 breaths per minute\nTemperature: 98.6°F\nOxygen saturation: 98% on room air\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In no distress.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nNeurological: Alert and oriented. No focal deficits.\nMusculoskeletal: Right ankle shows ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. There is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right ankle was reviewed and there is no fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nRight ankle sprain. \n\nPatient is a 48-year-old male who presents to the emergency department with right ankle pain after a fall on the ice. His symptoms are consistent with a right ankle sprain. \n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents with right ankle pain after a fall on the ice. He reports that he was walking out to take the trash and slipped on the ice. He denies numbness in his foot. He has been able to put a little weight on the foot but is still limping. He has been resting it and icing it. He has taken ibuprofen for the pain. He reports that it is a little better today. He has a past medical history significant for right ankle pain. The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with a right ankle sprain.\n\nTreatment: I advised the patient to keep his leg elevated and especially when seated. I also advised him to continue icing it. He was given an Aircast and crutches for the next 1-2 days. He will be able to start walking on it as tolerated. I advised him to continue taking NSAIDs as needed for pain.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow-up in 2 weeks to re-evaluate. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory is a pleasant 35-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right ankle pain.\n\nThe patient reports that he slipped on ice while taking out the trash and fell. He was able to get up with the assistance of a friend who was visiting him. He states that he was able to walk on it, but he is limping. The patient states that he has been keeping the ankle elevated and icing it. He states that he has been taking ibuprofen and that it is helping. The patient denies numbness in the foot. He states that he has been playing soccer for a long time and cannot recall injuring this ankle in the past.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been able to play soccer for the community league and is currently playing with his 4- and 5-year-old kids. His kids come to watch him play and his oldest son is old enough to play himself.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that he has been taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness in the foot.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In no acute distress.\nNeurological: Sensation is intact to light touch of the right foot. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right ankle reveals ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. There is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. There is no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness to palpation of the foot.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right ankle were reviewed and there is no fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient presents with a right ankle sprain.\n\nMedical Reasoning\n\nThe patient\'s symptoms are consistent with right ankle sprain. The patient\'s right ankle x-ray was normal, which is reassuring.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling\n\nI advised the patient to continue to elevate the right leg, especially when seated, and to ice the ankle. I advised the patient to use crutches for the next 1-2 days. I advised the patient that he may start walking as tolerated. I advised the patient to continue to take nsaids as needed for pain. I advised the patient to follow up in 2 weeks to re-evaluate his symptoms.\n\nPatient Agreements\n\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended plan of care.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks to re-evaluate his symptoms. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Hernandez is a pleasant 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of r