s to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain.\n\nThe onset of her knee pain started about 6 months ago. The patient denies any specific injury or trauma that caused the pain. She denies any trauma, injury or fall. She states that she has had the pain for 6 months. She states that it is a deep, aching pain that is behind her kneecaps. She denies any swelling or redness. She states that she can hear creaking in her knees. She denies any fever or chills. She states that she has tried to use ice and ibuprofen for her pain. She denies any past medical history of knee injuries. She denies any past surgeries. She denies any history of bone or joint problems.\n\nThe patient is a runner and states that she has decreased her running distance. She states that she can only run half a mile. She denies any pain with walking. She states that her pain is worse with standing. She states that she is unable to run. She denies any pain with stairs. She denies any pain with squatting.\n\nThe patient denies any numbness or tingling. She denies any tingling or numbness in her legs. She denies any muscle weakness. She denies any muscle weakness. She denies any loss of coordination.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient is married with two children. She works from home. She is a runner.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports knee pain. Neurological: Denies any numbness or tingling. Denies any tingling or numbness in her legs. Denies any muscle weakness. Denies any muscle weakness. Denies any loss of coordination. Respiratory: Denies any shortness of breath.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the Heart: Regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs.\n\nExamination of the Musculoskeletal: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for the lower extremities is 5/5. There is tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. There is positive patellar grind test. She is able to squat but it hurts. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are all negative bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the bilateral knees: No fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nBased on the patient\'s history and physical exam findings, her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a very common condition that we see that causes knee pain especially in active young people. This condition has to do with the way her kneecap which is her patella how that moves along the groove of her thigh her thigh bone which is the femur.\n\nThe patient may continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain. I am also going to recommend physical therapy where they will show her a number of lower extremity exercises and this will help increase her lower extremity strength her mobility and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have.\n\nI want to continue to assess her progress with each visit. I will be following up with her soon. I want her to take it easy for now from running. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. Her pain is aggravated by transitioning from a seated position to standing as well as ascending and descending stairs. Her pain is alleviated by rest. The patient has been icing her knees and wrapping her knees with an ACE bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally for pain. She states sh