d Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nAnna, a 26-year-old female, presents with a chief complaint of left elbow pain.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nAnna is a 26-year-old African American female who presents with a history of epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease. She reports left elbow pain that has been present for several months. The pain is located at the tip of the left elbow and is exacerbated by typing at her desk. She describes the pain as constant and severe, with a rating of 8/10. She also reports stiffness and swelling in the left elbow. She has difficulty opening and closing her left elbow due to the pain and stiffness. She has tried to manage the pain with over-the-counter medication, but it has not been effective. She is currently employed as a data entry clerk and works a standard 8-hour shift. She reports that the pain has been affecting her ability to perform her job and daily activities.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- General: Denies fever, chills, and weight loss.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain, stiffness, and swelling.\n- Neurological: Reports no seizures in the past year.\n- Gastrointestinal: Reports no nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n- Respiratory: Reports occasional nasal 