r rhonchi.\n- Cardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or edema.\n- Skin: No rash, hives, or swelling.\n- Musculoskeletal: No muscle weakness or joint pain.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Asthma, mild persistent, currently well-controlled on wixela 250/50, one puff in the morning and one puff at night.\n- Allergic Rhinitis, seasonal, well-controlled with zyrtec.\n- Food Allergy, peanut, well-controlled with avoidance.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- Continue wixela 250/50, one puff in the morning and one puff at night.\n- Continue zyrtec as needed during spring, summer, and fall.\n- Encourage the patient to try to reduce her wixela use to once daily if possible.\n- Consider stepping down to alvesco 80 mcg, two puffs twice daily if the wixela is not well-tolerated.\n- Perform a breathing test in four months to assess response to treatment.\n- Advise the patient to continue her daily routine of zyrtec during the spring, summer, and fall.\n- Encourage her to avoid peanuts and tree nuts, especially almonds and cashews.\n- Refill prescription for albuterol and epipen.\n- Refill prescription for zyrtec.\n- Recommend a follow-up in four months for a repeat breathing test and spirometry, sooner if needed.\n- Provide patient education on the importance of adherence to her medication regimen and the potential risks of not controlling her asthma.\n\n**Consent for Dax Documentation:**\nThe patient has given informed consent for the use of dax for documentation. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ History of present illness: The patient is a 20-year-old female who is being seen for her yearly follow-up for asthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy. She was switched to wixela due to insurance changes. She has been using it once in the morning and once at night and has not had any issues with it. She has been using zyrtec in the spring, summer and fall for her seasonal allergy symptoms which tend to work well. She has known peanut allergy and is tolerant to almonds and cashews. \nPast medical history: A