e possibly her blood pressure will improve significantly if she is able to completely stop consuming alcohol. Target BP is 130-140/70-80. I will see her back week of June 14 which likely will be the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nRecommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2, she defers this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic with any questions. Make follow up appointment week of June 14th.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\n- Hypertension\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\n- Amanda Taylor, a 72-year-old female, presents for follow-up regarding hypertension. She has a significant history of alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on April 16th, her blood pressure was 130/90 mmHg. She was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg PO QD and lisinopril 20 mg PO QD.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- General: Reports struggling with not drinking; wants to discuss it.\n- Cardiovascular: Reports no chest pain, shortness of breath, or palpitations.\n- Renal: Denies any swelling or edema.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports no joint pain or swelling.\n- Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or memory loss.\n- Endocrine: Reports no diabetes or thyroid issues.\n- Respiratory: Denies cough or shortness of breath.\n- Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or abdominal pain.\n- Genitourinary: Denies any issues.\n- Constitutional: Reports feeling generally well.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 150/100 mmHg\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Height: 5\'5"\n- Weight: 140 lbs\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- General: Alert, pleasant, in no acute distress.\n- Cardiovascular: Regular rate, no murmurs, no gallops, no jugular venous distension.\n- Respiratory: Clear to percussion and auscultation, no wheezing or rales.\n- Neurological: No tremors, no focal neurological deficits.\n- Musculoskeletal: No joint pain or swelling.\n- Skin: No rashes or lesions.\n- Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, non-distended.\n- Extremities: No edema.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Hypertension\n- Moderate alcohol use disorder\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- Continue Lisinopril 20 mg PO QD and Hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg PO QD.\n- Encourage and support cessation of alcohol use.\n- Provide information on the benefits of cessation, including improved blood pressure control.\n- Discussed the option of injectable Naltrexone for alcohol use disorder and the potential benefits of its use.\n- Started on injectable Naltrexone, to be administered on May 17th.\n- Discussed potential side effects and risks associated with Naltrexone, including nausea, headache, dizziness, anxiety, and insomnia.\n- Discussed the importance of regular follow-up and the potential benefits of abstinence.\n- Scheduled a follow-up appointment for June 14th, around the time of the second Naltrexone injection.\n- Recommended upgrading Influenza vaccination and Shingrix, which was deferred.\n- Discussed the importance of maintaining regular blood pressure checks and encouraged her to continue monitoring her blood pressure at home.\n- Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and the potential consequences of non-adherence.\n- Encouraged her to seek support from family and friends in maintaining abstinence.\n- Provided resources for local support groups and counseling services.\n- Discussed the importance of regular follow-up and the potential benefits of abstinence.\n- Discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle, including a balanced diet and regular exercise.\n\n**5. Referrals:**\n- None.\n\n**6. Patient Education:**\n- Provided patient with a clear and detailed explanation of the diagnosis and treatment plan.\n- Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and the potential consequences of non-adherence.\n- Discussed the importance of regular follow-up and the potential benefits of abstinence.\n- Encouraged her to seek support from family and friends in maintaining abstinence.\n- Provided resources for local support groups and counseling services.\n- Discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle, including a balanced diet and regular exercise.\n\n**7. Follow-up:**\n- Follow-up appointment scheduled for June 14th, around the time of the second Naltrexone injection.\n- Will review blood pressure and overall progress towards abstinence at this visit.\n- Will assess for any side effects or concerns related to Naltrexone and provide additional support and guidance as needed.\n\n**8. Additional Recommendations:**\n- Encourage patient to continue regular blood pressure checks and to monitor her blood pressure at home.\n- Discourage any further alcohol use.\n- Encourage patient to maintain a healthy lifestyle, including a balanced diet and regular exercise.\n- Discourage smoking and substance abuse.\n- Discourage any other risky behaviors.\n\n**9. Disposition:**\n- Discharged to follow-up with the provider on June 14th.\n\n**10. Referral to Other Professionals:**\n- None. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ HPC: 2022-04-28 14:30:00\nHPC: 2022-05-17 10:30:00\nHPC: 2022-06-14 14:30:00\nHPC: 2022-04-16\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02-09\nHPC: 2022-02