 having significant pain\n[patient] sounds good\n[doctor] okay thank you i\'m gon na send my nursing to see you and we\'ll get you out the door\n[patient] thank you\n[doctor] take care\n[patient] that one is a follow-up mm-hmm\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient\'s family owns and runs a bakery.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in feet.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes\n- Examination: No redness, discharge, or icterus.\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth, and Throat\n- Examination of Ears: Auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No pain to palpation of the upper back. Pain to palpation of the midline of the lower back, especially on the left. No pain to palpation on the right lower back. Mildly positive straight leg rise on the right Positive straight leg rise on the left.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe he has a musculoskeletal low back strain. I do not expect to see anything significant on x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed his diagnosis at length today. I explained that physical therapy can help with the pain, but also strengthen some of the muscles in the back. I explained that he needs to be careful while taking medications, such as an overuse of muscle relaxers if he is doing any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving.\n- Medical Treatment: Low back x-ray ordered. Referral to physical therapy provided. He will take 400 mg of ibuprofen up to 3 times per day. Tylenol can be used for severe pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nThe patient, a 45-year-old male, presents with a chief complaint of low back pain that has been present for about a week.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient reports that the pain started when he twisted his back while lifting a heavy object at home. He initially thought it was just a minor strain and tried over-the-counter medications, but the pain persisted. He reports that the pain is in the lower back, specifically in the middle and radiates to the left side. The pain worsens with sudden movements, particularly twisting to the left.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\nThe patient reports no other symptoms, but mentions that he has a history of diabetes and has been trying to manage it with diet and exercise. He takes metformin twice a day and tries to exercise regularly. He mentions that his blood sugar levels have been a bit higher lately, but he does not recall the exact numbers.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** The patient appears to be in mild discomfort, but in no acute distress.\n- **Vital Signs:** Blood Pressure: 125/80 mmHg, Heart Rate: 72 bpm, Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min, Temperature: 98.6°F, Height: 5\'9", Weight: 180 lbs.\n- **Constitutional:** No fever, no chills, no weight loss.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** The patient reports low back pain, specifically in the lower back, which worsens with sudden movements, particularly twisting to the left. There is tenderness to palpation in the lumbar region, particularly in the midline.\n- **Neurological:** No focal neurological deficits, no radiculopathy.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n**Diagnosis:**\n- **Musculoskeletal Low Back Strain:** The patient has a musculoskeletal low back strain, which is causing his symptoms of low back pain.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n**Medical Treatment:**\n- **Medications:** Ibuprofen 400 mg, to be taken as needed for pain relief.\n- **Prescription:** Flexeril 5 mg, to be taken once daily for muscle relaxation.\n\n**Referrals:**\n- **Physical Therapy:** Referral for physical therapy to improve flexibility and strength in the lower back.\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- **Follow-up Visit:** Follow-up in 2 weeks to assess response to treatment and to discuss the results of the low back x-ray.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- **Diet and Exercise:** Encouraged to continue diet and exercise to manage diabetes.\n- **Blood Sugar Monitoring:** Encouraged to monitor blood sugar levels regularly and to keep a record of the results.\n- **Lifestyle Recommendations:** Advised to avoid heavy lifting and bending to prevent exacerbating the low back strain.\n- **Patient Understanding:** The patient understands that the low back pain is likely due to a musculoskeletal strain and that the recommended treatment is to take ibuprofen as needed and to follow up in 2 weeks to reassess the condition.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\n- **Medication Adherence:** The patient agrees to take the prescribed medications as directed.\n- **Follow-up:** The patient agrees to follow up in 2 weeks to reassess the condition.\n\n**Patient Referrals:**\n- **Pain Management:** No referral to a pain management specialist at this time, but may be considered if the low back pain persists.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- **Diabetes Management:** The patient is reminded to continue managing his diabetes through diet and exercise and to follow up in 2 weeks to reassess his blood sugar levels.\n\n**Patient Compliance:**\n- **Medication Adherence:** The patient is advised to take the prescribed medications as directed and to follow up in 2 weeks to reassess the condition.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- **Low Back X-ray:** The patient will undergo a low back x-ray to assess the severity of the musculoskeletal strain.\n- **Follow-up Visit:** The patient will follow up in 2 weeks to reassess the condition and to discuss the results of the low back x-ray. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Patient\'s name: Noah\nDate of visit: \nAge: \nChief complaint: \nHPC: \nHPI: \nPMHx: \nSHx: \nDHx: \nFHx: \nDHx: \nSHx: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression: \nPlan: \nImpression