hat\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay alright well it was very nice seeing you today call me if you need anything\n[patient] i will\n[doctor] okay bye\n[patient] bye\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Price is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 2.5 weeks ago, when he stepped off of a curb and turned over his ankle. He locates his pain to the lateral aspect of his right knee. The patient also reports clicking with ambulation. He denies any pain with ambulation unless he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. The patient also reports locking of his knee with prolonged standing. He notices pain when he is resting his foot on his heel. He denies any pain at rest and elevated, or when his foot is flat on the ground. The patient states that he is more active during the day, which does not impact his knee pain. He adds that he is able to perform a full squat without pain. The patient reports numbness and tingling in his right foot, however this is not new. The patient has been taking ibuprofen, which provides him with relief at night. He has also been icing his knee 4 times per day, which provides him with relief in the morning and at the end of the day.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nHe states that he has fractured his 2nd toe 3 times over the last 10 years.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works in sales. He rides a bike for exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nNeurological: Positive for numbness and tingling in his toes on his right foot from prior injury.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: >Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Slight edema. Slight effusion. No pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full ROM. Negative Lachman\'s.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the right knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nThe patient, a 35-year-old Caucasian male, presents with a 2.5-week history of right knee pain and clicking sensation, which worsened after stepping off a curb.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient reports a sudden onset of right knee pain and clicking sensation after stepping off a curb 2.5 weeks ago. He describes the pain as mild to moderate, with occasional swelling and no fever. He states that the pain is more pronounced when he stands on one foot, particularly when leaning against the wall. He reports no redness, warmth, or pus. The patient has no history of trauma or previous knee problems. He denies any numbness or tingling in his foot, except for a history of broken second toe on his right foot three times over the last 10 years.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- General: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports pain and clicking sensation in the right knee, no palpable swelling or warmth.\n- Neurological: No numbness or tingling in the right foot, except for history of broken second toe on the right foot.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 125/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- General: Alert and cooperative, in no acute distress.\n- Musculoskeletal: Right knee: Slight edema and effusion noted. No pain to palpation on the lateral aspect. Full range of motion. Positive varus test. No pain to palpation on the medial aspect. No tenderness or swelling.\n- Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulses bilaterally.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- Right Knee X-ray: No acute fracture or bony abnormality noted.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Lateral collateral ligament strain, right knee.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Treatment:**\n- Prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg, oral, once daily, for 6 weeks.\n- Referral to physical therapy for strengthening exercises to improve muscle support around the right knee.\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- Follow-up in 2 weeks to assess for improvement in symptoms and monitor for any signs of complications.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Advised the patient on the nature of the injury and the need for physical therapy to strengthen the muscles around the knee.\n- Discussed the importance of proper footwear and proper lifting techniques to prevent further injury.\n- Instructed the patient on proper icing techniques and the use of Meloxicam as prescribed.\n- Encouraged the patient to maintain a healthy lifestyle, including regular exercise and proper nutrition.\n- Discussed the possibility of an MRI if symptoms persist or worsen.\n\n**Patient Agreement:**\n- The patient acknowledged the plan and agreed to follow the recommended treatment and follow-up. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ HPC: 2.5/5\nHx: \nP: 2.5/5\nPE: \nImp: Lateral collateral ligament strain\nPlan: \nDX: Lateral collateral ligament strain\nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nPlan: \nImp: \nP