 weakness observed.\n\t+ **Range of Motion:** Full range of motion in the ankle and foot.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- **Diagnosis:** Achilles Tendinitis.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Medical Treatment:**\n- Prescribed Ibuprofen 200 mg, 3 tablets, orally, every 6 hours as needed for pain relief.\n- Recommended to discontinue all high-impact activities for 2 weeks to allow for recovery.\n\n**Referrals:**\n- Referral to Physical Therapy for 2 sessions to address strength and flexibility issues.\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- Schedule follow-up appointment in 7-10 days after the first physical therapy session to monitor the patient\'s response to treatment and adjust the treatment plan as needed.\n\n**Additional Recommendations:**\n- Encourage patient to maintain a low-impact exercise routine during the 2-week recovery period.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Provided patient with a thorough explanation of the diagnosis and treatment plan.\n- Educated on the importance of rest and recovery for the achilles tendon.\n\n**Patient Agreement:**\n- The patient understands and agrees with the treatment plan and is willing to follow the recommendations.\n\n**Special Instructions:**\n- Patient is advised to monitor for any signs of worsening symptoms, such as increased pain or swelling, and to report any changes to the healthcare provider.\n\n**Follow-up Instructions:**\n- The patient is scheduled for a follow-up appointment in 7-10 days after the first physical therapy session to monitor the response to treatment and adjust the treatment plan as needed. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ HPC: 11/10/2022\n\nHPC: \nSubjective:\n- Patient is a 30-year-old male who is training for a triathlon\n- He complains of pain in the back of his heel and calf, which worsens after exercise\n- Pain is worse on the right side\n- Pain is worse after waking up and after prolonged sitting\n- Pain improves after 10-15 minutes of walking\n- No numbness or tingling\n- No weakness\n- No swelling\n- No redness\n- No fever\n- No