d to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\n- The patient, Alexis, presents with numbness and tingling in her right hand, which is exacerbated by typing on the keyboard.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\n- The patient is a 35-year-old female who works as a data entry clerk and has been experiencing symptoms for approximately 6 months. The symptoms have been persistent over the past month, with intermittent pain and numbness in her right hand, especially when typing on the keyboard. The numbness and tingling occur in the thumb, index, and middle fingers, with no pain in the other fingers. The symptoms are not severe enough to wake her up at night.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- The patient reports no other relevant symptoms in other body systems.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 118/78 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F (37°C)\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **Neurological:**\n\t+ Right hand: Decreased grip strength, numbness and tingling in the thumb, index, and middle fingers, especially on flexion and extension. Tinel\'s sign is positive.\n- **Musculoskeletal:**\n\t+ No other relevant findings.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- **Diagnosis:**\n\t+ Carpal Tunnel Syndrome (CTS)\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Treatment:**\n- Prescribed: Ibuprofen 600 mg, 4 times a day, to be taken orally.\n- Recommended: Wrist splint to be worn at night to help alleviate symptoms.\n- Ordered: Nerve Conduction Study (NCS) to confirm the diagnosis of CTS.\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- Scheduled for follow-up in 2 weeks to review the NCS results and assess the effectiveness of the treatment plan.\n- If the NCS results are positive, the patient will be referred to Physical Therapy to start conservative management, which may include exercises and therapy to improve range of motion and strength in the wrist and hand.\n- If the conservative management is not effective, the patient may be considered for surgery to release the compressed nerves in the wrist.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- The patient was informed about the diagnosis and the treatment plan, including the potential need for surgery if the conservative management is not effective.\n- The patient understands that the wrist splint will help alleviate symptoms and that the NCS will confirm the diagnosis.\n- The patient was advised to follow up in 2 weeks to review the NCS results and assess the effectiveness of the treatment plan.\n\n**Patient Agreement:**\n- The patient agreed to follow the treatment plan and understands the potential need for surgery if the conservative management is not effective. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ PC: Alexis\nHPC: 6 months\nPMHx: None\nDHx: None\nSHx: Data entry worker, using keyboard for 6-8 hours a day.  Numbness and tingling in R hand, more persistent over the last month. Pain worse with typing and using mouse. \nImp: Carpal tunnel syndrome\nPlan: \n1. Wrist splint at night\n2. Ibuprofen 600mg, 4 times a day\n3. Nerve conduction studies (EMG) to confirm diagnosis\n4. Follow-up with the doctor if the test is positive to discuss further treatment options, including physical therapy or surgery\nImp: Carpal tunnel syndrome\nImp: Carpal tunnel syndrome\nPlan: \n1. Wrist splint at night\n2. Ib