me.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on metformin 500 mg and order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate any need for medication adjustments.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nThe patient, a 55-year-old male, presents to the office with a chief complaint of right elbow pain that has been ongoing for about a week.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient reports that he has been experiencing sharp pain in his right elbow, specifically at the tip of the elbow, which is exacerbated by movement and radiates down to the forearm. He states that the pain is constant and has been present for about a week. He reports that the pain started after playing lacrosse with his kids, and he believes that his 13-year-old son may have hit him with the lacrosse stick. The patient reports that he tried taking Advil for the pain, but it has not provided significant relief.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **General:** Reports of sharp pain in the right elbow, no chest pain or shortness of breath.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports sharp pain in the right elbow, no other musculoskeletal symptoms reported.\n- **Endocrine:** Reports no other endocrine symptoms.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Reports no chest pain, no palpitations, no shortness of breath.\n- **Respiratory:** Reports no cough, no shortness of breath.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n- **Vital Signs:**\n\t+ Blood Pressure: 150/90 mmHg (high blood pressure)\n\t+ Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n\t+ Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n\t+ Temperature: 98.6°F (normal)\n- **Physical Exam:**\n\t+ **General:** Alert and cooperative.\n\t+ **Cardiovascular:** Grade 2 systolic ejection murmur noted.\n\t+ **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\t+ **Musculoskeletal:** Mild pain to palpation in the olecranon area of the posterior elbow, mild pain with flexion and extension, but normal range of motion.\n- **Imaging:**\n\t+ **X-ray:** No fracture or bony abnormality noted.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- **Diagnosis:**\n\t+ Elbow Contusion: The patient presents with a history of sharp pain in the right elbow, which is consistent with an elbow contusion. The physical examination findings of mild pain to palpation in the olecranon area and mild pain with flexion and extension, but normal range of motion, support this diagnosis.\n- **Other Conditions:**\n\t+ Hypertension: The patient has a history of hypertension, which is currently controlled with Lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\t+ Diabetes Mellitus: The patient has a history of diabetes, which is currently controlled with Metformin 500 mg daily.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Treatment:**\n\t+ **Elbow Contusion:**\n\t\t- Prescribed Ibuprofen 800 mg, to be taken twice a day for 2 weeks to help with pain and inflammation.\n\t\t- Advised to apply ice to the affected area for 20 minutes, 3 times a day for 2 weeks to help reduce swelling and pain.\n\t\t- Recommended avoiding activities that exacerbate the condition, such as playing lacrosse, for the next 2 weeks.\n- **Follow-up:**\n\t+ Will see the patient back in 2 weeks to reassess the elbow and adjust the treatment plan if necessary.\n- **Medication Adjustments:**\n\t+ No changes to the current medications for hypertension and diabetes at this time.\n- **Referrals:**\n\t+ None.\n- **Patient Education:**\n\t+ Provided patient education on the diagnosis and treatment of elbow contusion, including the importance of adhering to the treatment plan and avoiding activities that exacerbate the condition.\n- **Patient Agreement:**\n\t+ The patient understands and agrees with the treatment plan and follow-up recommendations.\n- **Follow-up Instructions:**\n\t+ Advised the patient to return for a follow-up visit in 2 weeks to reassess the elbow and adjust the treatment plan if necessary. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ HPC: 13yo\nHx of right elbow pain for 1 week, worse with f