ren between the ages of 9 to 11 years old and again between 17 to 20 years old be screened for their cholesterol. We also discussed that studies link high cholesterol to pre-mature heart attacks. I advised the patient that genetic testing can be done to see if the problem is genetic or a secondary cause. They were also advised to follow a balanced diet with a variety of foods that are low in saturated fat and sugars, as well as remain active.\n- Medical Treatment: Full cholesterol panel ordered. Hemoglobin A1c, CMP, thyroid study, and liver panel were also ordered. Genetic testing ordered. Referral to nutritionist provided.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow-up in 3 to 6 months after meeting the nutritionist for another cholesterol level.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nThe patient, Julie Cruz, a 17-year-old non-binary individual, presents for an initial consultation due to a newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient reports that they recently underwent an annual checkup and were informed of an elevated cholesterol level. They are concerned about the finding and would like to discuss the implications and potential management options.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- General: Reports no significant past medical history, no known allergies.\n- Cardiovascular: Reports no chest pain, palpitations, or shortness of breath.\n- Respiratory: No dyspnea, wheezing, or cough.\n- Gastrointestinal: No abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports enjoying outdoor activities like bike riding and hiking.\n- Endocrine: No thyroid issues reported.\n- Mental Status: No depression, anxiety, or sleep disturbances.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 124/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n- Height: 5\'8"\n- Weight: 140 lbs\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- General: Alert and cooperative, no acute distress.\n- Cardiovascular: No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n- Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- Eye Examination: No exophthalmos, no lid lag.\n- Neck Examination: No thyroid enlargement.\n- Abdominal Examination: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Hypercholesterolemia\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Recommendations:**\n- Order a full cholesterol panel to confirm the diagnosis.\n- Perform a genetic testing to determine if the hypercholesterolemia is familial or secondary.\n- Order a complete metabolic panel, glucose, and liver panels to rule out other potential causes.\n- Schedule a follow-up visit in 3-6 months to reassess the patient\'s cholesterol levels after dietary changes.\n- Refer the patient to a nutritionist to discuss a well-balanced diet and provide personalized dietary recommendations.\n- Encourage continued physical activity, such as bike riding, to maintain a healthy lifestyle.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Discussed the importance of lifestyle modifications to manage hypercholesterolemia.\n- Provided information on the benefits of a well-balanced diet and the role of genetics in hypercholesterolemia.\n- Emphasized the importance of regular follow-up visits to monitor cholesterol levels and adjust treatment as needed.\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- Schedule a follow-up visit in 3-6 months to reassess the patient\'s cholesterol levels and assess the effectiveness of dietary changes.\n\n**Treatment:**\n- No pharmacological treatment prescribed at this time. Dietary modifications and lifestyle changes are recommended.\n\n**Patient Agreement:**\n- The patient understands the diagnosis and treatment plan and agrees to follow the recommendations.\n\n**Consent:**\n- The patient provided informed consent for the recommended tests and procedures.\n\n**Referrals:**\n- Referral to a nutritionis